window.RTLY_DOCS = [{"t":"Quick start","u":"quick-start.html","d":"Install RTLY-Kit with Composer and, in about five minutes, convert a Jalali date, validate an Iranian national code and handle your first library exception.","g":"Getting started","h":[["What you will do","goal"],["1. Install","install"],["2. Your first date","first-date"],["3. Optional: short global names","globals"],["4. Your first validation","first-validation"],["5. Your first exception","first-exception"],["Things to know early","notes"],["Where to go next","next"]],"x":"What you will do In this page you install the package, print your first Jalali date, run your first validation and catch your first library exception. Everything runs on plain PHP; no framework, no configuration file and no extra dependency is needed. Prerequisites. PHP 8.2 or newer with the standard mbstring extension, and Composer. If you have neither PHP nor Composer on your machine, see the Docker route in Installation. 1. Install composer require enaxon/rtly-kit Composer installs the package and generates vendor/autoload.php. RTLY-Kit has no required Composer dependencies. Optional integrations (Carbon macros, Laravel) switch on by themselves when Carbon or Laravel is present. More in Installation. 2. Your first date Create a file quick.php next to vendor/: <?php declare(strict_types=1); require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; use function RtlyKit\\hdate; use function RtlyKit\\jdate; echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('l j F Y'), \"\\n\"; // شنبه 1 فروردین 1405 echo hdate('2026-03-21')->format('j F Y', 'en'), \"\\n\"; // 2 Shawwal 1447 Run it with php quick.php. The first line prints the Jalali date of 21 March 2026, which is Nowruz 1405. The second line shows the same day in the Hijri calendar. Two things to notice: The helper functions live in the RtlyKit namespace and are imported with use function. They can never collide with a function of your own or of another package. The string '2026-03-21' is read as a Gregorian date. A string such as '1405/01/01' (a year below 1700 in the Y/m/d form) is read as Jalali. See String semantics of make(). echo jdate('1405/01/01')->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'), \"\\n\"; // 2026-03-21 The helper returns a Jalali object (and hdate() a Hijri one, hebrew_date() a Hebrew one). These objects are immutable: addDays(), startOfMonth() and the other modifiers return a new object. 3. Optional: short global names By default no global function is defined. If you prefer plain jdate() and is_national_code(), opt in once, for example in your bootstrap file: $skipped = \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(); // array of names it could not define echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 register() defines the 27 helper names that are still free. It never overwrites an existing function and never throws; it returns the list of names it skipped (an empty array when everything was defined), and it is safe to call more than once. Use the namespaced form for any skipped name. Details: Helpers and globals. 4. Your first validation use function RtlyKit\\is_national_code; use function RtlyKit\\validate_national_code; use function RtlyKit\\to_persian_digits; use function RtlyKit\\number_to_words; var_dump(is_national_code('0013542419')); // bool(true) $result = validate_national_code('0013542410'); var_dump($result->isValid()); // bool(false) print_r($result->errors()); // [0] => invalid_checksum echo to_persian_digits('1405/01/01'), \"\\n\"; // ۱۴۰۵/۰۱/۰۱ echo number_to_words(1405), \"\\n\"; // یک هزار و چهارصد و پنج is_*() functions return a plain bool. The validate_*() functions return a Result with isValid(), stable machine-readable errors() keys and details(). Validators never throw for bad user input; they report it. See Validators overview and National code. 5. Your first exception Calendar code is different from validation: building an impossible date is a programming or data error, so the calendar classes throw. 1404 is not a leap year, so 30 Esfand 1404 does not exist: use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitThrowable; try { jdate('1404/12/30'); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo get_class($e), ': ', $e->getMessage(), ' [', $e->getErrorCode()->value, \"]\\n\"; } // RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException: Invalid Jalali date: 1404/12/30 [invalid_date] try { jdate('not a date'); } catch (RtlyKitThrowable $e) { echo 'library error: ', $e->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; // library error: Unable to parse date: not a date } Every exception of the library implements RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitThrowable and extends \\InvalidArgumentException, so one catch handles them all, and getErrorCode() gives a stable value to branch on (do not parse the message text). Out-of-range input, including years outside the supported ranges (Jalali -620 to 9377, Hijri 1 to 9665, Hebrew 3762 to 13759), always raises InvalidDateException, never a TypeError. More in Error handling. Things to know early Known limitation. The Jalali calendar here is the arithmetic 33-year rule, not the astronomical official calendar. The Hijri Umm al-Qura table (AH 1300 to 1500) comes from ICU/CLDR data and is not verified month by month against the official publication. Check Accuracy and data before using dates for legal or religious deadlines. Time zones. Without an explicit DateTimeZone, the PHP default zone is used. Pass a zone (for example new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran')) when the calendar day matters. Weekdays. Jalali numbers days from Saturday = 0; Hijri and Hebrew from Sunday = 0. Where to go next Dates: Jalali, Hijri, Hebrew, Convert and compare. Validation: Validators overview. Laravel projects: Laravel setup. Problems: Troubleshooting and FAQ."},{"t":"Installation","u":"installation.html","d":"Requirements, installation with Composer or Docker, optional Carbon and Laravel integration, supported versions, how to verify the install and how to remove the package cleanly.","g":"Getting started","h":[["Requirements","requirements"],["Install with Composer","composer"],["Optional packages","optional"],["Install without PHP on your machine (Docker)","docker"],["Supported versions","versions"],["Verify the installation","verify"],["If something fails","problems"],["Uninstall","uninstall"]],"x":"Requirements Requirement Status Notes PHP ^8.2 Required Tested in CI on PHP 8.2, 8.3 and 8.4 ext-mbstring Required Enabled in standard PHP builds; used by ordinal(), Slugify and the text tools Composer 2 Required to install Handles autoloading; the package has no required Composer dependency nesbot/carbon ^3.0 Optional Enables the Carbon macros illuminate/support ^11 or ^12 Optional Laravel auto-discovery, facade, validation rules and the Eloquent cast (Laravel setup) That is all. In particular, ext-calendar, ext-intl and any database are not needed: the three calendars are implemented in pure PHP, and the Umm al-Qura table ships inside the package. Install with Composer composer require enaxon/rtly-kit Make sure your entry script loads Composer's autoloader, once: <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; The autoloader maps the RtlyKit\\ namespace to the package's src/ directory and also loads one helper file that defines the namespaced helper functions (RtlyKit\\jdate() and the others). No global function is defined by that file; see Helpers and globals for the opt-in short names. Optional packages composer require nesbot/carbon # Carbon macros: toJalali(), createFromJalali(), ... composer require illuminate/support # only in non-Laravel projects that want the Laravel pieces Both integrations activate on their own. The Carbon macros register when the autoloader finds Carbon. In a Laravel application the service provider and the Jalali facade alias are found by package auto-discovery, so nothing is added to config/app.php. After installing into an existing Laravel project run php artisan package:discover if your deployment caches discovery. Install without PHP on your machine (Docker) If PHP and Composer are not installed locally, run them from official images. From your project directory: docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/app -w /app composer:2 require enaxon/rtly-kit docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/app -w /app php:8.3-cli php quick.php The first command installs the package into vendor/; the second runs a script such as the one in Quick start with PHP 8.3 (the official CLI image includes mbstring). To work on the library itself, clone the repository and use its docker-compose.yml: docker compose -f tools/docker-compose.yml run --rm php composer install docker compose -f tools/docker-compose.yml run --rm php composer test docker compose -f tools/docker-compose.yml run --rm php php -r 'require \"vendor/autoload.php\"; echo RtlyKit\\jdate(\"2026-03-21\")->format(\"Y/m/d\");' Supported versions PHP 8.2, 8.3 and 8.4 (CI matrix). Newer 8.x releases are expected to work but are not yet in the matrix. Laravel 11 and 12. Carbon 3. Versioning. Before 1.0.0 a minor release may contain breaking changes. Each one is listed in the upgrade guide with migration steps (Upgrade), and the compatibility promise and the list of internal classes are in API stability. Until 1.0.0, pin the minor version in your composer.json constraint (a tilde constraint such as ~0.x.y with the version you tested) and read the upgrade guide before raising it. Verify the installation Create verify.php in your project root: <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; echo PHP_VERSION, \"\\n\"; echo extension_loaded('mbstring') ? \"mbstring: yes\\n\" : \"mbstring: NO\\n\"; echo \\RtlyKit\\jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 Run php verify.php. The last line must print 1405/01/01; a PHP version below 8.2 or mbstring: NO means the environment does not meet the requirements. Two Composer commands help as well: composer show enaxon/rtly-kit # installed version and metadata composer check-platform-reqs # checks PHP and extensions against every installed package If something fails Class \"RtlyKit\\...\" not found or Call to undefined function RtlyKit\\jdate(): vendor/autoload.php was not required, or Composer's autoloader is stale; run composer dump-autoload. Call to undefined function jdate() (without the namespace): the short global names are opt-in. Use use function RtlyKit\\jdate; or call \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(). Composer refuses to install and mentions the PHP version or ext-mbstring: install PHP 8.2 or newer, or enable mbstring in php.ini. More cases: Troubleshooting and FAQ. Uninstall Remove the package: composer remove enaxon/rtly-kit. This also regenerates the autoloader. Delete your own usages: use RtlyKit\\... and use function RtlyKit\\... lines, calls to \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(), and any catch blocks for RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\*. Search your code for RtlyKit to find them all. Carbon projects: the macros (toJalali, jformat, toHijri, toHebrew, createFromJalali, createFromHijri, createFromHebrew) disappear with the package; replace calls to them first. Laravel projects: remove the national_code, sheba, bank_card, iran_mobile, mobile, postal_code and vehicle_plate validation rules from your form requests, the JalaliCast from your models, any use of the Jalali facade, and any translation files you copied from the package. Then run php artisan optimize:clear. Data your application already wrote to the database is not touched by removing the package, so check how a cast stored it before you drop the cast. The package installs nothing outside vendor/ (apart from the Composer lock and autoload metadata), so there is nothing else to clean up."},{"t":"Jalali calendar","u":"jalali.html","d":"The immutable Jalali (Persian, Solar Hijri) date class: create and parse dates, format them with PHP date tokens, shift and snap them, compare and diff them, with the exact year range and error behaviour.","g":"Calendars","h":[["What the class does","overview"],["Creating dates","make"],["From components: create()","create"],["From anything: make()","make-values"],["String semantics of make()","string-rules"],["With a format: createFromFormat()","from-format"],["Supported range and errors","range"],["Reading values","getters"],["Weekday numbering","weekdays"],["Formatting","format"],["Shifting and snapping","modify"],["Comparing and measuring","compare-diff"],["Static helpers","static"],["Practical notes","notes"]],"x":"What the class does RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali is an immutable date-time value in the Persian (Solar Hijri) calendar. Internally it wraps one DateTimeImmutable instant, so it has a time zone, a Unix timestamp and an exact Gregorian counterpart; the Jalali year, month and day are computed from that instant. Every modifier returns a new object and the original never changes. The class is final and implements the shared CalendarDate contract described in Convert and compare dates. Prerequisites. Install the package (Installation). All examples assume this header: <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException; use function RtlyKit\\jdate; // namespaced helper, same as Jalali::make() Known limitation. The conversion implements the arithmetic 33-year-cycle rule (leap years at residues 1, 5, 9, 13, 17, 22, 26, 30 modulo 33). It is not the astronomical calendar used for the official Iranian calendar. For the modern years it agrees with the official calendar, but the two rules are different by construction and may disagree on a leap day in some far past or future year. Do not use this class as an authority for legal deadlines that depend on the official publication. Creating dates From components: create() create($year, $month, $day, $hour = 0, $minute = 0, $second = 0, $timezone = null) builds a date from Jalali components. The date is validated against the real month length of that year, so 1404/12/30 (1404 is not a leap year) is rejected while 1403/12/30 is accepted. Without a time zone the PHP default time zone (date_default_timezone_get()) is used. $tehran = new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran'); $d = Jalali::create(1404, 7, 15, 14, 30, 5, $tehran); echo $d; // 1404/07/15 14:30:05 echo $d->getTimestamp(); // 1759834805 echo $d->toGregorian()->format('c'); // 2025-10-07T14:30:05+03:30 From anything: make() Jalali::make($time = null, $timezone = null) accepts a DateTimeInterface, another CalendarDate (Hijri, Hebrew, Jalali), an int Unix timestamp, a string or null (now). The namespaced helper jdate() is a thin alias of it. $utc = new DateTimeZone('UTC'); echo Jalali::make(new DateTimeImmutable('2026-03-21 12:00', $utc)); // 1405/01/01 12:00:00 echo Jalali::make(0, $utc); // 1348/10/11 00:00:00 echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('l j F Y'); // شنبه 1 فروردین 1405 echo Jalali::now(); // current instant in the default time zone echo Jalali::today(); // today at 00:00:00 String semantics of make() Strings are handled in this order: Persian and Arabic digits are converted to English digits, and the string is trimmed. An empty or whitespace-only string throws InvalidDateException. If the string looks like Y/m/d or Y-m-d with a 3 or 4 digit year, optionally followed by H:i or H:i:s (separated by a space or T), and the year is below 1700, it is read as a Jalali date. Every other string, including any year of 1700 or later, is passed to DateTimeImmutable and read as Gregorian or free-form text ('2025-10-07', 'tomorrow', '10 October 2025'). echo Jalali::make('1403/12/30'); // 1403/12/30 00:00:00 echo Jalali::make('۱۴۰۴/۰۷/۱۵'); // 1404/07/15 00:00:00 (Persian digits) echo Jalali::make('1404-07-15 08:05'); // 1404/07/15 08:05:00 echo Jalali::make('2025-10-07')->format('Y/m/d'); // 1404/07/15 (Gregorian text) echo Jalali::make('1700/01/01')->format('Y/m/d'); // 1078/10/12 (1700 or later is Gregorian) Watch the 1700 boundary. A string such as '0001-01-01' or '1500-06-01' is read as a Jalali date, not as a Gregorian one. To pass a real Gregorian date earlier than year 1700, build a DateTimeImmutable yourself and hand that object to make(). Invalid input always raises InvalidDateException: foreach (['1404/12/30', '1404/13/01', '1404/07/15 25:00', '', 'garbage'] as $s) { try { Jalali::make($s); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo \"'$s': \", $e->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; } } // '1404/12/30': Invalid Jalali date: 1404/12/30 // '1404/13/01': Invalid Jalali date: 1404/13/1 // '1404/07/15 25:00': Invalid time: 25:0:0 // '': Unable to parse an empty date string. // 'garbage': Unable to parse date: garbage With a format: createFromFormat() Jalali::createFromFormat($format, $time, $timezone = null) parses with the PHP DateTime::createFromFormat token syntax (digits may be Persian) but interprets the year, month and day as Jalali values. Gregorian-only limits such as \"February has at most 29 days\" do not apply; the Jalali calendar validates the result. echo Jalali::createFromFormat('Y/m/d H:i', '1404/07/15 08:30', $tehran); // 1404/07/15 08:30:00 echo Jalali::createFromFormat('d-m-Y', '31-06-1404'); // 1404/06/31 00:00:00 try { Jalali::createFromFormat('Y/m/d', 'abc'); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(); // Unable to parse 'abc' with format 'Y/m/d' } Supported range and errors Supported Jalali years are Jalali::MIN_YEAR = -620 to Jalali::MAX_YEAR = 9377. This is exactly the span that maps to Gregorian years 1 to 9999: the first supported day is -620/01/01 (0001-03-21) and the last is 9377/12/30 (9999-03-20). Every entry point checks the range and throws only InvalidDateException (never a TypeError, ValueError or DateMalformed* exception), including create(), make(), createFromFormat(), integer timestamps, add*()/sub*() and the static converters. foreach ([[-621, 1, 1], [9378, 1, 1], [1404, 12, 30], [1404, 0, 1], [1404, 7, 31]] as [$y, $m, $d]) { try { Jalali::create($y, $m, $d); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; } } // Invalid Jalali date: -621/1/1 // Invalid Jalali date: 9378/1/1 // Invalid Jalali date: 1404/12/30 // Invalid Jalali date: 1404/0/1 // Invalid Jalali date: 1404/7/31 Time components are checked too: Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1, 24, 0, 0) throws Invalid time: 24:0:0. An integer timestamp outside Gregorian years 1 to 9999 throws Timestamp out of the supported range: .... Catch InvalidDateException to handle only calendar problems, or the base class RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitException (also the marker"},{"t":"Hijri calendar","u":"hijri.html","d":"The immutable Hijri (Islamic) date class with the Umm al-Qura month table and an arithmetic Tabular variant: create, parse and format dates in Arabic, Persian or English, shift, compare and diff them.","g":"Calendars","h":[["What the class does","overview"],["Variants: Umm al-Qura and Tabular","variants"],["Table coverage","table-coverage"],["Creating dates","make"],["create()","create"],["make() and the helper","make-values"],["String semantics","string-rules"],["Supported range and errors","range"],["Reading values","getters"],["Weekday numbering","weekdays"],["Formatting","format"],["Shifting and snapping","modify"],["Comparing and measuring","compare-diff"],["Calendar arithmetic","static"],["Practical notes","notes"]],"x":"What the class does RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Hijri is an immutable date-time value in the Islamic (Hijri) calendar. Like the other calendar classes it wraps one DateTimeImmutable instant and implements the shared CalendarDate contract (see Convert and compare dates). What is specific to Hijri is the variant: the rule set that decides how long each month is. Every instance carries its variant, and every modifier keeps it. All examples assume this header (see Installation): <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Hijri; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\HijriVariant; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException; use function RtlyKit\\hdate; // namespaced helper, same as Hijri::make() $utc = new DateTimeZone('UTC'); Variants: Umm al-Qura and Tabular Variant How month lengths are decided HijriVariant::UmmAlQura (default) An embedded month-length table for AH 1300 to 1500 (1882-11-12 to 2077-11-16). Outside it the Tabular rules are used as a fallback. HijriVariant::Tabular The arithmetic civil calendar: a 30-year cycle with 11 leap years. Deterministic for every year, but it can differ from Umm al-Qura by one or two days. $uq = Hijri::create(1446, 10, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc); // Umm al-Qura $tab = Hijri::create(1446, 10, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc, HijriVariant::Tabular); echo $uq->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'); // 2025-03-30 echo $tab->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'); // 2025-03-31 echo $uq->getVariant()->name; // UmmAlQura Two further anchors: 1 Ramadan 1446 is 2025-03-01 and 1 Muharram 1447 is 2025-06-26 (Umm al-Qura). Known limitation. The Umm al-Qura table (AH 1300 to 1500) was generated from the ICU/CLDR islamic-umalqura calendar data and spot-checked against known dates. It has not been verified month by month against the official KACST publication. Neither variant reproduces moon-sighting calendars (used in Iran, Morocco and elsewhere), which can differ by a day. Day boundaries follow civil midnight, not sunset. Do not use the result for religious observance dates that are announced by an authority. Table coverage Outside AH 1300 to 1500, create() and make() do not throw: they extrapolate with the Tabular rules, and the two rule sets may not join seamlessly at the border. Use Hijri::hasUmmAlQuraData($year) and $date->usesUmmAlQuraTable() to find out which rule actually applied. echo Hijri::hasUmmAlQuraData(1299) ? 1 : 0; // 0 echo Hijri::hasUmmAlQuraData(1300) ? 1 : 0; // 1 echo Hijri::hasUmmAlQuraData(1500) ? 1 : 0; // 1 echo Hijri::hasUmmAlQuraData(1501) ? 1 : 0; // 0 $o = Hijri::create(1600, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc); echo $o->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'); // 2173-12-06 var_dump($o->usesUmmAlQuraTable()); // bool(false) (tabular extrapolation) var_dump(Hijri::create(1446, 9, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc)->usesUmmAlQuraTable()); // bool(true) Creating dates create() Hijri::create($year, $month, $day, $hour = 0, $minute = 0, $second = 0, $timezone = null, $variant = HijriVariant::UmmAlQura) validates the date against the real month length in the chosen variant. $h = Hijri::create(1446, 9, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc); echo $h; // 1446/09/01 00:00:00 echo $h->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'); // 2025-03-01 var_dump($h->usesUmmAlQuraTable()); // bool(true) make() and the helper Hijri::make($time = null, $timezone = null, $variant = HijriVariant::UmmAlQura) accepts a DateTimeInterface, any CalendarDate, an int Unix timestamp, a string or null (now). The helper hdate($time, $timezone) calls it with the default variant. Hijri::now() and Hijri::today() take ($timezone, $variant). echo Hijri::make('2025-03-01', $utc); // 1446/09/01 00:00:00 echo hdate('2025-03-01')->format('j F Y', 'en'); // 1 Ramadan 1446 echo Hijri::make(Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc)); // 1446/09/21 00:00:00 echo Hijri::make('2025-03-31', $utc)->format('Y/m/d'); // 1446/10/02 echo Hijri::make('2025-03-31', $utc, HijriVariant::Tabular)->format('Y/m/d'); // 1446/10/01 (The Jalali line needs use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali;.) String semantics Persian and Arabic digits become English digits and the string is trimmed; empty strings throw InvalidDateException. A string of the form Y/m/d or Y-m-d (3 or 4 digit year, optional H:i[:s]) with a year below 1700 is read as a Hijri date in the chosen variant. Everything else, including years of 1700 or later, is parsed as Gregorian or free-form text. echo Hijri::make('1446/09/01', $utc); // 1446/09/01 00:00:00 (Hijri) echo Hijri::make('1700/01/01', $utc); // 1111/07/10 00:00:00 (Gregorian 1700-01-01) try { Hijri::make('1446/02/31'); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(); } // Invalid Hijri date: 1446/2/31 Variant of a Hijri argument. Hijri::make($hijriInstance) returns that very instance unchanged and ignores the $variant argument. To re-express a date in another variant, go through the instant: Hijri::make($h->toGregorian(), null, HijriVariant::Tabular). Supported range and errors Hijri years Hijri::MIN_YEAR = 1 to Hijri::MAX_YEAR = 9665 are supported: the first day is 1/1/1 (0622-07-19) and the last is 9665/12/last (9999-10-01), i.e. Gregorian years 622 to 9999. Anything outside throws InvalidDateException from create(), make(), integer timestamps, add*()/sub*() and the converters; never a TypeError or ValueError. foreach ([[0, 1, 1], [9666, 1, 1], [1446, 1, 31], [1446, 13, 1]] as [$y, $m, $d]) { try { Hijri::create($y, $m, $d); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; } } // Invalid Hijri date: 0/1/1 // Invalid Hijri date: 9666/1/1 // Invalid Hijri date: 1446/1/31 // Invalid Hijri date: 1446/13/1 try { Hijri::make(new DateTimeImmutable('0600-01-01', $utc)); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(); } // Date out of the supported Hijri range (1..9665): -22 try { Hijri::create(9665, 1, 1)->addYears(1); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(); } // Hijri year out of the supported range: 9666 Reading values The getters are those of the contract: getYear(), getMonth(), getDay(), getHour(), getMinute(), getSecond(), getTimestamp(), getTimezone(),"},{"t":"Hebrew calendar","u":"hebrew.html","d":"The immutable Hebrew (Jewish) date class with ordinal month numbering, leap-year Adar I and II, and month names in English, Hebrew and Persian: create, parse, format, shift and measure dates.","g":"Calendars","h":[["What the class does","overview"],["Month numbering","months"],["Creating dates","make"],["String semantics","string-rules"],["Supported range and errors","range"],["Reading values","getters"],["Weekday numbering","weekdays"],["Formatting","format"],["Shifting and snapping","modify"],["Comparing and measuring","compare-diff"],["Calendar arithmetic","static"]],"x":"What the class does RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Hebrew is an immutable date-time value in the Hebrew (Jewish) calendar. It is pure PHP (no ext-calendar) and implements the fixed arithmetic calendar: the molad of Tishrei, the four dehiyyot postponements, and year lengths of 353/354/355 days (regular) and 383/384/385 days (leap). Like the other calendars it wraps one DateTimeImmutable instant and implements the CalendarDate contract (see Convert and compare dates). <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Hebrew; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException; use function RtlyKit\\hebrew_date; // namespaced helper, same as Hebrew::make() $utc = new DateTimeZone('UTC'); Known limitation. A Hebrew day really begins at sunset. This class, like the other calendars of the library, changes the date at civil midnight, so an evening time (for example the eve of a holiday) belongs to the previous Hebrew date in religious practice but to the next civil day here. The class also does not compute holidays, parashot or Omer counts. Month numbering Months are numbered by their ordinal position in the year starting from Tishrei (the civil new year). In a leap year, Adar I and Adar II are two separate months: Months 1 to 5 are always Tishrei, Cheshvan, Kislev, Tevet, Shevat. Regular year: 6 = Adar, 7 = Nisan, 8 = Iyar, 9 = Sivan, 10 = Tammuz, 11 = Av, 12 = Elul. Leap year: 6 = Adar I, 7 = Adar II, 8 = Nisan, 9 = Iyar, 10 = Sivan, 11 = Tammuz, 12 = Av, 13 = Elul. Not Hebcal numbering. This differs from Hebcal (Nisan = 1) and from PHP ext-calendar. Elul is month 12 in a regular year and 13 in a leap year, and Nisan is month 7 or 8. Always get month numbers from Hebrew itself (getMonth(), monthName(), monthsInYear()), never from tables of another library. foreach ([5784, 5785] as $y) { $a = []; for ($m = 1; $m <= Hebrew::monthsInYear($y); $m++) { $a[] = $m.':'.Hebrew::monthName($y, $m).'('.Hebrew::daysInMonth($y, $m).')'; } echo $y, ' ', implode(' ', $a), \"\\n\"; } // 5784 1:Tishrei(30) 2:Cheshvan(29) 3:Kislev(29) 4:Tevet(29) 5:Shevat(30) 6:Adar I(30) 7:Adar II(29) 8:Nisan(30) 9:Iyar(29) 10:Sivan(30) 11:Tammuz(29) 12:Av(30) 13:Elul(29) // 5785 1:Tishrei(30) 2:Cheshvan(30) 3:Kislev(30) 4:Tevet(29) 5:Shevat(30) 6:Adar(29) 7:Nisan(30) 8:Iyar(29) 9:Sivan(30) 10:Tammuz(29) 11:Av(30) 12:Elul(29) Hebrew::monthName($year, $month, $locale = 'en') supports en, he and fa (unknown locales use en); in a regular year slot 6 is the plain Adar. Creating dates Hebrew::create($year, $month, $day, $hour = 0, $minute = 0, $second = 0, $timezone = null) validates against the real length of that month in that year (Cheshvan and Kislev vary between 29 and 30 days). $e = Hebrew::create(5785, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc); echo $e; // 5785/01/01 00:00:00 echo $e->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'); // 2024-10-03 echo Hebrew::create(5785, 7, 15, 0, 0, 0, $utc)->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'); // 2025-04-13 (15 Nisan 5785) echo Hebrew::create(5786, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, $utc)->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d l'); // 2025-10-02 Thursday Hebrew::make($time = null, $timezone = null) accepts a DateTimeInterface, any CalendarDate, an int timestamp, a string or null; hebrew_date() is the helper. Passing a Hebrew instance returns it unchanged. echo Hebrew::make('2025-10-07', $utc); // 5786/01/15 00:00:00 echo hebrew_date('2025-10-07')->format('Y/m/d F'); // 5786/01/15 Tishrei echo Hebrew::make('5785/01/10', $utc); // 5785/01/10 00:00:00 echo Hebrew::make('0001-09-06')->format('Y/m/d'); // 3762/01/01 String semantics Persian and Arabic digits become English digits; the string is trimmed; an empty string throws InvalidDateException. A string of the form Y/m/d or Y-m-d (optional H:i[:s]) with a year of 3000 or more is read as a Hebrew date, using the ordinal month numbering above. (The threshold is opposite to Jalali and Hijri, which read years below 1700 as their own.) Every other string is parsed as Gregorian or free-form text. So '5785/01/10' is Hebrew, '2025-10-07' is Gregorian, and '3000/01/01' is read as Hebrew year 3000, which is below the supported minimum and therefore throws Invalid Hebrew date: 3000/1/1. Supported range and errors Hebrew years Hebrew::MIN_YEAR = 3762 to Hebrew::MAX_YEAR = 13759 are supported. These are exactly the years that lie inside Gregorian years 1 to 9999: the first day is 1 Tishrei 3762 (0001-09-06) and the last is the last day of Elul 13759 (9999-11-03). Anything outside throws InvalidDateException; isValid() returns false instead. foreach ([[3761, 1, 1], [13760, 1, 1], [5785, 13, 1], [5785, 3, 31]] as [$y, $m, $d]) { try { Hebrew::create($y, $m, $d); } catch (InvalidDateException $x) { echo $x->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; } } // Invalid Hebrew date: 3761/1/1 // Invalid Hebrew date: 13760/1/1 // Invalid Hebrew date: 5785/13/1 (5785 is a regular year: 12 months) // Invalid Hebrew date: 5785/3/31 echo Hebrew::create(5784, 13, 1) instanceof Hebrew ? 'ok' : ''; // ok (5784 is a leap year) try { Hebrew::make('0001-01-01'); } catch (InvalidDateException $x) { echo $x->getMessage(); } // Date out of the supported Hebrew range (3762..13759): 3761 The pure arithmetic helpers daysInYear(), daysInMonth() and monthName() accept years 1 to 13759 (year 0 throws Hebrew year out of the supported range: 0); only date construction uses the narrower 3762 minimum. Reading values Getters are those of the contract (getYear(), getMonth(), getDay(), time parts, getTimestamp(), getTimezone(), toGregorian(), toDateString(), toDateTimeString()). getMonth() is the ordinal month described above. Weekday numbering getDayOfWeek() and the w token are Sunday-first: 0 = Sunday to 6 = Saturday (like Hijri and PHP, unlike Jalali). N is the ISO number (Monday = 1). echo $e->getDayOfWeek(); // 4 (1 Tishrei 5785 was a Thursday) foreach (range(0, 6) as $i) { echo $e->addDays($i)->getDayOfWeek(); } // 4560123 Formatting format($pattern = 'Y/m/d H:i:s', $locale = 'en') with $locale one of en, he, fa. Digits are always Latin. A backslash escapes the next character. echo"},{"t":"Convert and compare dates","u":"convert-and-compare.html","d":"The shared CalendarDate contract of Jalali, Hijri and Hebrew, how to convert a date between calendars, and how comparison and differences work across calendars and time zones.","g":"Calendars","h":[["One instant, three calendars","idea"],["Converting between calendars","convert"],["Pass a date to another class's make()","make-from-calendar"],["From and to Gregorian","gregorian"],["Time zones decide the date","time-zones"],["The CalendarDate contract","contract"],["Comparing across calendars","compare"],["Differences across calendars","diff"],["Edge cases and limits","limits"]],"x":"One instant, three calendars The three calendar classes (Jalali, Hijri, Hebrew) share one design: each wraps a single DateTimeImmutable instant and only views it in its own calendar. Conversion between calendars therefore never goes through text; it is the same instant read through another set of rules. Two consequences follow from this: Converting a date and converting back gives the same instant. Comparing or subtracting dates of different calendars is always well defined, because they are compared as instants. All examples use this header: <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Hebrew; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Hijri; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali; use RtlyKit\\Contracts\\CalendarDate; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException; $utc = new DateTimeZone('UTC'); Converting between calendars Pass a date to another class's make() Every make() accepts a CalendarDate (as well as a DateTimeInterface, an int timestamp, a string or null). Pass the date you have to the class you want: $j = Jalali::create(1405, 1, 1, 12, 0, 0, $utc); echo Hijri::make($j)->format('Y/m/d F', 'en'); // 1447/10/02 Shawwal echo Hebrew::make($j)->format('Y/m/d F'); // 5786/07/03 Nisan echo Jalali::make(Hijri::make($j)); // 1405/01/01 12:00:00 The time of day and the time zone are carried over. To re-express the result in another zone pass a DateTimeZone as the second argument of make(): $tehran = new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran'); $h = Hijri::make($j, $tehran); echo $h->getTimezone()->getName(); // Asia/Tehran echo $h->getHour(), ':', $h->getMinute(); // 15:30 (12:00 UTC) Same-class shortcut. Hijri::make($hijri) and Hebrew::make($hebrew) return the very instance you pass (any time zone or variant argument is ignored). Jalali::make($jalali) returns it too unless you pass a time zone, in which case you get a copy in that zone. To change a Hijri date's variant, go through the instant: Hijri::make($h->toGregorian(), null, HijriVariant::Tabular). From and to Gregorian toGregorian() returns the underlying DateTimeImmutable; passing a DateTimeInterface to make() goes the other way. The static converters work on plain integers: $g = new DateTimeImmutable('2026-03-21 12:00', $utc); echo json_encode([ Jalali::gregorianToJalali(2026, 3, 21), Hijri::gregorianToHijri(2026, 3, 21), Hebrew::gregorianToHebrew(2026, 3, 21), ]); // [[1405,1,1],[1447,10,2],[5786,7,3]] echo json_encode(Jalali::jalaliToGregorian(1405, 1, 1)); // [2026,3,21] echo get_class($j->toGregorian()); // DateTimeImmutable The Gregorian year must be 1 to 9999. Each calendar's own range (Jalali -620 to 9377, Hijri 1 to 9665, Hebrew 3762 to 13759) is the part of that span the calendar can represent, so converting an early Gregorian date to Hebrew or Hijri can throw InvalidDateException even though the Gregorian date itself is valid: try { Hebrew::make(new DateTimeImmutable('0001-01-01', $utc)); } catch (InvalidDateException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(); } // Date out of the supported Hebrew range (3762..13759): 3761 Time zones decide the date The calendar date of an instant depends on the zone you look at it in. The same moment is still 1 Farvardin 1405 (21 March) in UTC but already 2 Farvardin in Tehran, which is 3.5 hours ahead: $instant = new DateTimeImmutable('2026-03-21 22:30', $utc); echo Jalali::make($instant); // 1405/01/01 22:30:00 (UTC) echo Jalali::make($instant, $tehran); // 1405/01/02 02:00:00 (Tehran, +03:30) Pass an explicit zone whenever the calendar day matters; do not rely on the server default. The CalendarDate contract RtlyKit\\Contracts\\CalendarDate is an interface that extends Stringable and is implemented by Jalali, Hijri and Hebrew. Type-hint it to write code that works with every calendar: function describe(CalendarDate $d): string { return sprintf('%s %d/%02d/%02d', (new ReflectionClass($d))->getShortName(), $d->getYear(), $d->getMonth(), $d->getDay()); } foreach ([Jalali::class, Hijri::class, Hebrew::class] as $class) { echo describe($class::make($g)), \"\\n\"; } // Jalali 1405/01/01 // Hijri 1447/10/02 // Hebrew 5786/07/03 Group Members Construction make(), now(), today(), create() Calendar arithmetic (static) isValid(), isLeapYear(), daysInYear(), daysInMonth() Getters getYear(), getMonth(), getDay(), getHour(), getMinute(), getSecond(), getDayOfWeek(), getTimestamp(), getTimezone(), toGregorian() Formatting format(), toDateString(), toDateTimeString(), __toString() Modification add/sub for Days, Hours, Minutes, Seconds, Months, Years; startOf/endOf for Day, Month, Year Comparison eq, ne, gt, gte, lt, lte, equals, isBefore, isAfter, between, isPast, isFuture, isToday Difference diffInDays(), diffInMonths(), diffInYears() Guarantees that hold for every implementation: Instances are final and immutable; modifiers return a new object. Every method that takes a date, timestamp or delta throws only InvalidDateException for out-of-range input, never a TypeError or ValueError. format() is declared with the pattern only; each class adds its own optional trailing parameters ($persianDigits for Jalali; $locale and $digits for Hijri; $locale for Hebrew). Through the interface you can call only format($pattern). Weekday numbering is not uniform: Jalali is Saturday-first, Hijri and Hebrew are Sunday-first. $i = new DateTimeImmutable('2025-10-07', $utc); // a Tuesday echo Jalali::make($i)->getDayOfWeek(), ' ', Hijri::make($i)->getDayOfWeek(), ' ', Hebrew::make($i)->getDayOfWeek(); // 3 2 2 (Jalali: Saturday = 0; the others: Sunday = 0) Comparing across calendars All comparison methods take CalendarDate|DateTimeInterface and compare timestamps. No conversion is needed first: $nowruz = Jalali::create(1405, 1, 1, 12, 0, 0, $utc); var_dump($nowruz->eq(Hijri::make($nowruz))); // bool(true) var_dump($nowruz->eq($nowruz->toGregorian())); // bool(true) var_dump($nowruz->gt(Hebrew::create(5785, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc))); // bool(true) var_dump($nowruz->between(Hijri::create(1447, 8, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc), Hebrew::create(5787, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, $utc))); // bool(true)"},{"t":"Carbon macros","u":"carbon-macros.html","d":"Optional Carbon integration: convert any Carbon instance to Jalali, Hijri or Hebrew and create Carbon dates from calendar components, with the exact macro list, return types and errors.","g":"Calendars","h":[["What this gives you","overview"],["How registration works","registration"],["Macro reference","list"],["From Carbon to a calendar","to-calendar"],["From calendar components to Carbon","from-calendar"],["Errors","errors"],["Limits and cautions","known"]],"x":"What this gives you If nesbot/carbon is installed, RTLY-Kit registers a small set of macros on Carbon\\Carbon and Carbon\\CarbonImmutable. They let you move between Carbon and the calendar classes in one call, in both directions. Carbon is optional: RTLY-Kit does not require it, and without Carbon nothing is registered and nothing breaks. Laravel applications already ship Carbon, so the macros are available there with no extra step (see also Laravel setup). Prerequisites. RTLY-Kit installed (Installation) and Carbon 3 (composer require nesbot/carbon; Carbon 3 is the supported version). How registration works Composer loads the package's helper file on autoload. That file boots the integration, which checks whether the Carbon classes exist and, if so, registers the macros once. You do not call anything yourself: <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use Carbon\\Carbon; use Carbon\\CarbonImmutable; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\HijriVariant; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali; var_dump(Carbon::hasMacro('toJalali')); // bool(true) Install Carbon with Composer. The macros are registered when Composer's autoloader starts and Carbon's classes can be found. A Carbon copy that is not on the same autoloader (for example a bundled phar) is not detected. The registration classes are internal; rely on the macro names, which are part of the public API. Macro reference Macro Kind Returns Notes toJalali() instance Jalali Same instant and time zone as the Carbon object jformat($format = 'Y/m/d H:i:s') instance string Shortcut for toJalali()->format($format); tokens as in Jalali formatting toHijri(?HijriVariant $variant = null) instance Hijri Umm al-Qura unless a variant is given toHebrew() instance Hebrew createFromJalali($y, $m, $d, $h = 0, $i = 0, $s = 0, $tz = null) static the class you called it on Carbon:: returns Carbon; CarbonImmutable:: returns CarbonImmutable createFromHijri($y, $m, $d, $h = 0, $i = 0, $s = 0, $tz = null, ?HijriVariant $variant = null) static the class you called it on Variant defaults to Umm al-Qura createFromHebrew($y, $m, $d, $h = 0, $i = 0, $s = 0, $tz = null) static the class you called it on Ordinal month numbering, see Hebrew months The $tz argument accepts a DateTimeZone, a time zone identifier string, or null (then the PHP default zone applies, not the zone of any existing Carbon object). From Carbon to a calendar $c = Carbon::create(2026, 3, 21, 12, 0, 0, 'UTC'); echo $c->toJalali(); // 1405/01/01 12:00:00 echo get_class($c->toJalali()); // RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali echo $c->jformat('l j F Y'); // شنبه 1 فروردین 1405 echo $c->jformat(); // 1405/01/01 12:00:00 echo $c->toHijri()->format('j F Y', 'en'); // 2 Shawwal 1447 echo $c->toHebrew()->format('j F Y'); // 3 Nisan 5786 The result is a calendar object, not a Carbon object, so use the calendar API from there (Persian digits, for example, via $c->toJalali()->format('Y/m/d', true)). Because a calendar object is a normal value, you can keep working with it and go back at any time with toGregorian(): echo Carbon::now('UTC')->setDate(2026, 3, 21)->toJalali()->addMonths(1)->format('Y/m/d'); // 1405/02/01 Calling the calendar's make() directly with a Carbon instance is equivalent, because Carbon is a DateTimeInterface: echo Jalali::make($c)->format('Y/m/d H:i'); // 1405/01/01 12:00 echo Jalali::make(Carbon::create(2026, 3, 21, 22, 0, 0, 'UTC'), new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran'))->format('Y/m/d H:i'); // 1405/01/02 01:30 From calendar components to Carbon $a = Carbon::createFromJalali(1405, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 'UTC'); echo get_class($a), ' ', $a->toDateTimeString(); // Carbon\\Carbon 2026-03-21 00:00:00 $b = CarbonImmutable::createFromJalali(1405, 1, 1, 8, 30, 0, 'Asia/Tehran'); echo get_class($b), ' ', $b->toIso8601String(); // Carbon\\CarbonImmutable 2026-03-21T08:30:00+03:30 echo Carbon::createFromHijri(1446, 9, 1, 0, 0, 0, 'UTC')->toDateString(); // 2025-03-01 echo Carbon::createFromHebrew(5786, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 'UTC')->toDateString(); // 2025-09-23 Pass the Tabular Hijri variant as the last argument of createFromHijri(): Carbon::createFromHijri(1446, 10, 1, 0, 0, 0, 'UTC', HijriVariant::Tabular). Errors The macros validate through the calendar classes, so invalid or out-of-range input throws RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException (never a Carbon InvalidFormatException for these cases), and so does an unknown time zone identifier: use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitThrowable; $cases = [ fn () => Carbon::createFromJalali(1404, 12, 30), fn () => Carbon::createFromJalali(1404, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 'Nowhere/Land'), fn () => Carbon::createFromHijri(9999, 1, 1), fn () => Carbon::createFromHebrew(1, 1, 1), ]; foreach ($cases as $f) { try { $f(); } catch (RtlyKitThrowable $e) { echo get_class($e), ': ', $e->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; } } // RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException: Invalid Jalali date: 1404/12/30 // RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException: Unknown timezone: Nowhere/Land // RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException: Invalid Hijri date: 9999/1/1 // RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException: Invalid Hebrew date: 1/1/1 Supported ranges are those of the calendars: Jalali -620..9377, Hijri 1..9665, Hebrew 3762..13759. Limits and cautions Known limitation. The macros inherit the accuracy of the calendar classes: Jalali is the arithmetic 33-year rule (not the astronomical official calendar), and Hijri Umm al-Qura data for AH 1300 to 1500 comes from ICU/CLDR and is not verified month by month against the official publication. Day boundaries are civil midnight. Macros are global per class: if another package defines a macro with the same name (toJalali, toHijri ...), whichever registers last wins. Use the calendar classes directly (Jalali::make($carbon)) when you need certainty. Time zones travel with the object: toJalali() uses the Carbon object's own zone, so normalise the zone before converting if the calendar day matters. Weekday numbers differ between calendars; do not feed getDayOfWeek() of one calendar into another. See Convert and compare dates."},{"t":"Validators overview","u":"validators-overview.html","d":"How the six Iranian validators work: the shared Validator contract, the Result object, stable error keys, input normalization and size limits.","g":"Validation","h":[["The six validators","six-validators"],["Two ways to call","two-ways"],["The Validator contract","contract"],["The Result object","result"],["Detail keys per validator","detail-keys"],["Error keys","error-keys"],["Input handling","input-handling"],["Normalization","normalization"],["Lookup tables and their provenance","lookups"],["Where to go next","next"]],"x":"The six validators RTLY-Kit ships six validators for data that Iranian applications handle every day. They all follow one shape, so once you know one you know them all. Class Checks Page NationalCode 10 digits, mod-11 check digit, place-of-issue hint National code Sheba IR + 24 digits, ISO 7064 mod-97-10, bank lookup Sheba and bank card BankCard 16 digits, Luhn checksum, bank lookup by BIN Sheba and bank card Mobile Shape of 09xxxxxxxxx in five input forms, operator hint Mobile, postal code, plate PostalCode 10 digits, first digit not 0 Mobile, postal code, plate VehiclePlate Passenger-car plate shape, parsed into parts Mobile, postal code, plate All of them live in the RtlyKit\\Validation namespace and accept Persian and Arabic digits as typed by users, so you can pass form input straight in without cleaning it first. Two ways to call Every validator offers a plain boolean and a structured result. Use isValid() when you only need yes or no, and validate() when you want to tell the user why a value was rejected. <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\NationalCode; use function RtlyKit\\is_national_code; var_dump(NationalCode::isValid('۰۴۹۹۳۷۰۸۹۹')); // bool(true) var_dump(is_national_code('0499370899')); // bool(true) $result = NationalCode::validate('0499370898'); var_dump($result->isValid()); // bool(false) print_r($result->errors()); // Array ( [0] => invalid_checksum ) The helpers (is_national_code(), validate_sheba(), and so on) are namespaced; import them with use function RtlyKit\\is_national_code;. Short global names are opt-in; see Helpers and globals. The Validator contract Every validator implements RtlyKit\\Contracts\\Validator, which has exactly two static methods: validate(mixed $value): Result isValid(mixed $value): bool, a shortcut for validate($value)->isValid() Validators are stateless, so the methods are static. That makes it easy to pick a validator at runtime through a class-string, for example from a configuration array: use RtlyKit\\Contracts\\Validator; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\Mobile; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\NationalCode; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\PostalCode; /** @var array<string, class-string<Validator>> $rules */ $rules = [ 'national_code' => NationalCode::class, 'mobile' => Mobile::class, 'postal' => PostalCode::class, ]; $input = ['national_code' => '0499370899', 'mobile' => '0912', 'postal' => '1234567890']; foreach ($rules as $field => $class) { $result = $class::validate($input[$field]); echo $field, ': ', $result->isValid() ? 'ok' : implode(',', $result->errors()), \"\\n\"; } // national_code: ok // mobile: invalid_format // postal: ok Never throws. Neither method throws for bad input. Any value, including null, arrays, objects, oversized strings and invalid UTF-8, produces an invalid Result with a stable error key. Exceptions are reserved for programmer errors elsewhere in the library; see Error handling. The Result object RtlyKit\\Validation\\Result is an immutable value object with three read methods: Method Returns isValid() bool errors() list<string>: stable error keys, empty when valid details() array<string, mixed>: facts the validator learned while checking The details are present on both valid and invalid results (as far as the validator got before it stopped), so you can display the normalized value or a bank name even next to an error: use RtlyKit\\Validation\\BankCard; $result = BankCard::validate('6037991234567890'); var_dump($result->isValid()); // bool(false) print_r($result->errors()); // Array ( [0] => invalid_checksum ) echo json_encode($result->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"6037991234567890\",\"bin\":\"603799\",\"bank_name\":\"بانک ملی ایران\"} Detail keys per validator Validator Detail keys NationalCode normalized, location ({province, city} or null) Sheba normalized, bank_code, bank_name BankCard normalized, bin, bank_name Mobile normalized, operator PostalCode normalized VehiclePlate normalized, and when the shape matched: two_digit, letter, three_digit, region Lookup values (location, bank_name, operator) are null when the table has no entry. A missing entry means \"unknown\", never \"invalid\". Error keys Errors are stable, machine-readable keys. They do not change between releases, so you can map them to your own messages or translations. The Laravel integration does exactly that; see Laravel validation and cast. Key Meaning Used by invalid_type The value is not a string, an int, or an integral finite float all input_too_long A string longer than 4096 bytes all invalid_format The shape is wrong (character set, digit count, pattern) all except PostalCode, which uses it for a leading 0 invalid_length The digit count is not 10 PostalCode repeated_digits Every digit is the same (1111111111) NationalCode, BankCard invalid_checksum The check digit or check digits do not match NationalCode, Sheba, BankCard invalid_region A numeric part of the plate is all zeros VehiclePlate A result carries at most one error today (the first check that failed), but errors() returns a list so that future checks can report several. Input handling Before any rule runs, the value goes through one shared gate: Strings up to 4096 bytes pass. Persian and Arabic digits take two bytes each, so the cap is about 2000 such digits. A longer string yields input_too_long. int is converted with (string). float must be finite, integral, and below 1015 in magnitude; it is converted without an exponent. NaN, INF, 1.5 and huge floats yield invalid_type. Everything else, namely null, bool, arrays and objects, yields invalid_type. use RtlyKit\\Validation\\NationalCode; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\PostalCode; print_r(NationalCode::validate(null)->errors()); // [invalid_type] print_r(NationalCode::validate(1.5)->errors()); // [invalid_type] print_r(NationalCode::validate(str_repeat('1', 4097))->errors()); // [input_too_long] print_r(NationalCode::validate(\"\\xff\\xfe\")->errors()); // [invalid_format] var_dump(PostalCode::validate(1234567890)->isValid()); // bool(true)"},{"t":"National code","u":"national-code.html","d":"Validate Iranian national codes (کد ملی) with the mod-11 check digit, accept Persian and Arabic digits, and read the place-of-issue hint safely.","g":"Validation","h":[["Quick start","quick-start"],["What is checked","what-is-checked"],["The check-digit algorithm","algorithm"],["Accepted input","accepted-input"],["The Result details","result"],["Place-of-issue hint","location"],["What validation does not tell you","what-is-not-checked"],["Using it in a form","in-forms"]],"x":"Quick start <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\NationalCode; var_dump(NationalCode::isValid('0499370899')); // bool(true) var_dump(NationalCode::isValid('۰۴۹۹۳۷۰۸۹۹')); // bool(true), Persian digits var_dump(NationalCode::isValid('049-937-0899')); // bool(true), hyphens removed $result = NationalCode::validate('0499370899'); echo json_encode($result->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"0499370899\",\"location\":{\"province\":\"تهران\",\"city\":\"شهرری\"}} The class implements the shared Validator contract, so validate() returns a Result and nothing throws for bad input. The helpers are RtlyKit\\is_national_code() and RtlyKit\\validate_national_code(): use function RtlyKit\\is_national_code; use function RtlyKit\\validate_national_code; var_dump(is_national_code('۰۴۹۹۳۷۰۸۹۹')); // bool(true) print_r(validate_national_code('0499370898')->errors()); // Array ( [0] => invalid_checksum ) What is checked Checks run in this order and stop at the first failure: Type and size. Strings, int and integral float are accepted; anything else is invalid_type, and a string over 4096 bytes is input_too_long. Normalization. Persian and Arabic digits become English; spaces, hyphens and half-spaces (ZWNJ) are removed and the ends are trimmed. Format. Exactly 10 digits, otherwise invalid_format. Repeated digits. 0000000000 to 9999999999 pass the arithmetic by coincidence but are not real codes, so they return repeated_digits. Check digit (the mod-11 rule below), otherwise invalid_checksum. The check-digit algorithm Multiply each of the first nine digits by a weight from 10 down to 2, add the products, and take the remainder of the sum divided by 11. If the remainder is 0 or 1, the check digit equals the remainder; otherwise it is 11 minus the remainder. For 0499370899: the products are 0, 36, 72, 63, 18, 35, 0, 24 and 18, which add up to 266. The remainder of 266 divided by 11 is 2, so the check digit must be 11 − 2 = 9, and the last digit is indeed 9. print_r(NationalCode::validate('0499370898')->errors()); // [invalid_checksum] print_r(NationalCode::validate('1111111111')->errors()); // [repeated_digits] print_r(NationalCode::validate('12345')->errors()); // [invalid_format] print_r(NationalCode::validate('abc')->errors()); // [invalid_format] Keep it a string. A national code can start with zero. NationalCode::validate(499370899) receives the nine digits 499370899 and returns invalid_format. Do not cast the value to an integer anywhere between the input and the validator, including in a database column or a JSON number. Accepted input Input Result '0499370899' valid '۰۴۹۹۳۷۰۸۹۹' (Persian digits) valid '٠٤٩٩٣٧٠٨٩٩' (Arabic-Indic digits) valid '049-937-0899', ' 0499370899 ' valid; separators and spaces removed null, [], 1.5 invalid_type 12.0 read as '12', so invalid_format 4097 characters input_too_long Only spaces, hyphens and ZWNJ are treated as separators. Slashes, dots and letters are not removed and cause invalid_format. To get the cleaned form without validating, call NationalCode::normalize(): echo NationalCode::normalize(' ۰۴۹-۹۳۷ ۰۸۹۹ '); // 0499370899 The Result details Key Type Meaning normalized string The code after normalization (empty for invalid_type / input_too_long) location array or null {province, city} for a valid code whose prefix is in the table, otherwise null Error keys: invalid_format, repeated_digits, invalid_checksum, invalid_type, input_too_long. The meaning of each is in the error key table. Place-of-issue hint The first three digits of a national code are a prefix assigned by the civil registry. NationalCode::getLocation() maps it to a province and a city, and returns null when the code is invalid or the prefix is not in the table: print_r(NationalCode::getLocation('0499370899')); // Array ( [province] => تهران [city] => شهرری ) var_dump(NationalCode::getLocation('0499370898')); // NULL (invalid code) $a = NationalCode::validate('0012345679'); // prefix 001 echo json_encode($a->details()['location'], JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"province\":\"تهران\",\"city\":\"تهران مرکزی\"} $b = NationalCode::validate('9991234561'); // valid checksum, prefix 999 not in the table var_dump($b->isValid(), $b->details()['location']); // bool(true) NULL A hint, not proof. The prefix identifies where the original birth certificate or card was issued, not where the person was born or lives. Many people hold codes issued elsewhere, and province boundaries have changed since issuance (Karaj, for example, appears under Tehran). Never use the location to verify identity, origin or residence. Known limitation. The civil registry publishes no machine-readable list. The table has 547 prefixes, each kept only when three community datasets agree on both province and city and a fourth does not contradict it. These datasets share ancestry (several are ports or derivatives of one another), so their agreement is strong but not fully independent. A missing prefix means unknown, not invalid. See Accuracy and data for the sources. What validation does not tell you A valid checksum means the number is well formed. It does not mean the code was ever issued or belongs to a given person. Legal entities use a different 11-digit identifier. It is not handled by this validator and is rejected as invalid_format. Foreign nationals' identification numbers (Amayesh and similar) are a different scheme and are not covered. Using it in a form Show the user a message chosen by the error key, and store the normalized value: $messages = [ 'invalid_format' => 'The national code must be 10 digits.', 'repeated_digits' => 'This national code is not valid.', 'invalid_checksum' => 'This national code is not valid.', ]; $result = NationalCode::validate($_POST['national_code'] ?? null); if (! $result->isValid()) { $key = $result->errors()[0]; echo $messages[$key] ?? 'Invalid input.'; } else { $code = $result->details()['normalized']; // save this value } In Laravel, use the ready-made rule and translations instead;"},{"t":"Sheba and bank card","u":"sheba-and-bank-card.html","d":"Validate Sheba (IBAN) numbers with ISO 7064 mod-97-10 and bank card numbers with the Luhn checksum, and look up the issuing bank with honest data caveats.","g":"Validation","h":[["Quick start","quick-start"],["Sheba (IBAN)","sheba"],["Format","sheba-format"],["Normalization","sheba-normalization"],["What is checked","sheba-checks"],["Bank card number","bank-card"],["Format and checks","card-format"],["Bank lookup data","data"],["Practical notes","notes"]],"x":"Quick start <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\BankCard; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\Sheba; use function RtlyKit\\is_bank_card; use function RtlyKit\\is_sheba; var_dump(Sheba::isValid('IR800170000000000000000123')); // bool(true) var_dump(is_sheba('800170000000000000000123')); // bool(true), \"IR\" added for you var_dump(BankCard::isValid('6037-9912-3456-7893')); // bool(true) var_dump(is_bank_card('۶۰۳۷۹۹۱۲۳۴۵۶۷۸۹۳')); // bool(true), Persian digits echo Sheba::getBankName('IR800170000000000000000123'), \"\\n\"; // بانک ملی ایران echo BankCard::getBankName('6219861234567898'), \"\\n\"; // بانک سامان The numbers in these examples are synthetic: they pass the checksum but do not belong to real accounts. Both classes implement the shared Validator contract, never throw for bad input, and also have the helpers validate_sheba() and validate_bank_card(). Sheba (IBAN) Format A Sheba number is the Iranian IBAN: IR, two check digits, and a 22-digit BBAN, 26 characters in all. The first three digits of the BBAN identify the bank. IR 80 017 0000000000000000123 │ │ │ └─ rest of the BBAN (19 digits) │ │ └──── bank code (3 digits) │ └─────── check digits └────────── country code Normalization Sheba::normalize() upper-cases the text, converts Persian and Arabic digits, and removes spaces, hyphens and half-spaces. A bare string of exactly 24 digits, the common \"without IR\" form that banks print, gets the IR prefix. Any other input is left as typed and then fails the format check: echo Sheba::normalize('800170000000000000000123'); // IR800170000000000000000123 echo Sheba::normalize('ir80 0170 0000'); // IR8001700000 echo json_encode(Sheba::validate('ir80-0170-0000-0000-0000-0001-23')->isValid()); // true What is checked Type and size (invalid_type, input_too_long). Format: IR followed by exactly 24 digits, otherwise invalid_format. Checksum: ISO 7064 mod-97-10. The first four characters move to the end, I and R become 18 and 27, and the resulting number modulo 97 must equal 1; otherwise invalid_checksum. The bank lookup runs before the checksum, so the bank code is present in details() even for a number with a wrong check digit: $r = Sheba::validate('IR060170000000000000000123'); var_dump($r->isValid()); // bool(false) print_r($r->errors()); // Array ( [0] => invalid_checksum ) echo $r->details()['bank_name']; // بانک ملی ایران $ok = Sheba::validate('IR800170000000000000000123'); echo json_encode($ok->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"IR800170000000000000000123\",\"bank_code\":\"017\",\"bank_name\":\"بانک ملی ایران\"} $unknown = Sheba::validate('IR049990000000000000000123'); var_dump($unknown->isValid(), $unknown->details()['bank_name']); // bool(true) NULL Details: normalized, bank_code (three-digit string, or null when the format failed), bank_name (or null when the code is not in the table). A checksum-valid Sheba with an unknown bank code is valid; the name is simply missing. Bank names. Names are those of the issuing bank at assignment time. Codes of banks that later merged keep their original name. Bank card number Format and checks An Iranian bank card has 16 digits. BankCard::normalize() keeps only the digits after converting Persian and Arabic ones, so spaces, hyphens and other separators are free. The checks, in order: Type and size. Exactly 16 digits, otherwise invalid_format. All 16 digits identical (for example 0000000000000000) gives repeated_digits. The Luhn checksum, otherwise invalid_checksum. Starting from the right, every second digit is doubled (subtracting 9 when the result exceeds 9), and the digit sum must be divisible by 10. $ok = BankCard::validate('6037-9912 3456 7893'); echo json_encode($ok->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"6037991234567893\",\"bin\":\"603799\",\"bank_name\":\"بانک ملی ایران\"} print_r(BankCard::validate('6037991234567890')->errors()); // [invalid_checksum] print_r(BankCard::validate('0000000000000000')->errors()); // [repeated_digits] print_r(BankCard::validate('1234')->errors()); // [invalid_format] $x = BankCard::validate('9999991234567893'); // checksum ok, BIN not in the table var_dump($x->isValid(), $x->details()['bank_name']); // bool(true) NULL Details: normalized, bin (first six digits, or null if the format failed), bank_name. As with Sheba, the BIN lookup runs before the digit-repetition and checksum checks. BankCard::getBankName() and Sheba::getBankName() are shortcuts that return the name only for a valid number, and null otherwise: var_dump(BankCard::getBankName('6037991234567890')); // NULL, because the checksum fails var_dump(BankCard::getBankName('6037991234567893')); // string(26) \"بانک ملی ایران\" Bank lookup data Table Key Entries Card BINs first 6 digits of the card 39 Sheba bank codes 3 digits after IRkk 38 An entry is included only when at least two independent sources agree on the mapping (public bank-prefix tables and community datasets). Single-source or conflicting entries are left out on purpose. Known limitation. The tables are small and best-effort. Many valid cards come from a bank that is not listed, and for those bank_name is null. Some of the community sources share ancestry, so their agreement is strong but not fully independent, and the official Central Bank of Iran table was not reachable when the data was compiled (a few Sheba codes are confirmed by two sources only). Banks merge, rename and reassign ranges, so the data was checked on 2026-10-07 and will drift. Treat the name as a display hint; never use it for legal or financial decisions. Details are in Accuracy and data. Validity never depends on these tables. A checksum-valid number is valid whether or not its bank is known. Practical notes The checksum catches typos, not fake numbers. It cannot tell you that a card exists or that an account belongs to someone. Validate the card number for shape only. Never log full card numbers, and do not store the CVV2 or the second password. Store the normalized value, so 6037-9912-3456-7893 and"},{"t":"Mobile, postal code and plate","u":"mobile-postal-plate.html","d":"Validate Iranian mobile numbers in every common input form, postal codes, and vehicle plates, with operator hints, parsing and honest limits.","g":"Validation","h":[["Quick start","quick-start"],["Mobile numbers","mobile"],["Accepted forms","mobile-forms"],["The rule","mobile-rules"],["Operator hint","mobile-operator"],["Postal code","postal-code"],["Vehicle plate","vehicle-plate"],["Shape","plate-shape"],["Letters","plate-letters"],["Errors","plate-errors"]],"x":"Quick start <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\Mobile; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\PostalCode; use RtlyKit\\Validation\\VehiclePlate; use function RtlyKit\\is_mobile; use function RtlyKit\\is_postal_code; use function RtlyKit\\is_vehicle_plate; var_dump(is_mobile('+98 912 123 4567')); // bool(true) var_dump(is_postal_code('۱۲۳۴۵-۶۷۸۹۰')); // bool(true) var_dump(is_vehicle_plate('12ب345 ایران 67')); // bool(true) echo Mobile::getOperator('09121234567'), \"\\n\"; // همراه اول All three implement the shared Validator contract. Their helpers are validate_mobile(), validate_postal_code() and validate_vehicle_plate(). Mobile numbers Accepted forms Mobile normalizes everything to the national form 09xxxxxxxxx (11 digits). It accepts these five spellings, with Persian or Arabic digits and with spaces, hyphens or parentheses mixed in: Input normalized 09121234567 09121234567 9121234567 09121234567 989121234567 09121234567 +989121234567 09121234567 00989121234567 09121234567 ۰۹۱۲ ۱۲۳ ۴۵۶۷ 09121234567 (0912) 123-4567 09121234567 Because Mobile keeps only digits, any non-digit separator is dropped. An integer such as 9121234567 is also accepted (it has no leading zero to lose, so it is read as the 10-digit form). The rule Validity is a shape check: after normalization the number must match 09 followed by a digit from 0 to 4 or 9, then eight more digits. These are the blocks 090x to 094x and 099x that are allocated to mobile networks. Anything else, such as 0950... or a 10-digit number, returns invalid_format. $r = Mobile::validate('09121234567'); echo json_encode($r->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"09121234567\",\"operator\":\"همراه اول\"} print_r(Mobile::validate('09501234567')->errors()); // [invalid_format] print_r(Mobile::validate('0912123456')->errors()); // [invalid_format] echo Mobile::normalize('+98 912 123 4567'); // 09121234567 Operator hint details()['operator'] and Mobile::getOperator() look the number up in a small table of well-established prefixes: Hamrah-e Aval (0910-0919, 0990-0994), Irancell (0900-0905, 0930, 0933, 0935-0939), RighTel (0920-0922), Shatel Mobile (0998) and Aptel (09991). The longest matching prefix wins. For a valid number whose prefix is not in the table the operator is null: echo Mobile::getOperator('09351234567'), \"\\n\"; // ایرانسل echo Mobile::getOperator('09981234567'), \"\\n\"; // شاتل موبایل echo Mobile::getOperator('09991012345'), \"\\n\"; // آپتل var_dump(Mobile::validate('09951234567')->isValid()); // bool(true) var_dump(Mobile::getOperator('09951234567')); // NULL, valid but not in the table var_dump(Mobile::getOperator('09501234567')); // NULL, invalid number Known limitation. The operator is a prefix hint only. Number portability lets people keep their number when they change operator, so the prefix never guarantees the current operator. The table is deliberately conservative: blocks whose allocation differs between sources (for example 0931, 0932, 0934, 0940-0949 and most of 0995-0999) are omitted and return null, though the number is still valid. The table has no individually cited source; see Accuracy and data. Do not route SMS or billing by it. Details: normalized, operator. The only error keys are invalid_format, invalid_type and input_too_long. Landline and international numbers are not accepted. Postal code An Iranian postal code has 10 digits and never starts with 0. PostalCode keeps only digits (Persian and Arabic digits are converted first, then separators such as spaces and hyphens are dropped) and checks the length and the first digit: $r = PostalCode::validate('۱۲۳۴۵-۶۷۸۹۰'); echo json_encode($r->details()); // {\"normalized\":\"1234567890\"} print_r(PostalCode::validate('12345')->errors()); // [invalid_length] print_r(PostalCode::validate('123456789012')->errors()); // [invalid_length] print_r(PostalCode::validate('0123456789')->errors()); // [invalid_format] Error Meaning invalid_length The digit count is not 10 invalid_format The code starts with 0 invalid_type, input_too_long See Input handling Shape only. There is no check digit in a postal code, and no region-range check is performed: a 10-digit code that does not start with 0 is valid even if Iran Post has not assigned it. An integer such as 1234567890 is accepted because a valid code never starts with zero, but keeping postal codes as strings is still the safer habit. Vehicle plate Shape VehiclePlate understands the common passenger-car plate: two digits, one letter, three digits, and a two-digit region code, written as 12ب345 ایران 67. The word «ایران», spaces, hyphens, underscores and half-spaces are ignored, so these all mean the same plate: echo VehiclePlate::normalize('۱۲ ب ۳۴۵ ایران ۶۷'); // 12ب34567 $r = VehiclePlate::validate('12ب345 ایران 67'); echo json_encode($r->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"12ب34567\",\"two_digit\":\"12\",\"letter\":\"ب\",\"three_digit\":\"345\",\"region\":\"67\"} print_r(VehiclePlate::parse('12ب345 ایران 67')); // Array ( [two_digit] => 12 [letter] => ب [three_digit] => 345 [region] => 67 ) var_dump(VehiclePlate::parse('xx')); // NULL Letters The letter must be one of: الف ب پ ت ث ج د ز س ش ص ط ع ف ق ک گ ل م ن و ه ی ژ. The long form الف counts as one letter. The Latin D and S are accepted for diplomatic and special plates. Arabic letter variants are normalized first, so ك and ي are read as ک and ی: echo json_encode(VehiclePlate::validate('12ي34567')->details(), JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE); // {\"normalized\":\"12ی34567\",\"two_digit\":\"12\",\"letter\":\"ی\",\"three_digit\":\"345\",\"region\":\"67\"} var_dump(VehiclePlate::isValid('12D34567')); // bool(true) var_dump(VehiclePlate::isValid('12الف34567')); // bool(true) Errors invalid_format: the shape does not match (wrong digit counts, a letter that is not allowed, a missing part). invalid_region: the shape matches, but the region is 00, the two-digit part is 00, or the three-digit part is 000. The parsed parts are still in details(). print_r(VehiclePlate::validate('12ب345')->errors()); //"},{"t":"Digits and number formatting","u":"digits-and-format.html","d":"Convert between English, Persian and Arabic-Indic digits, format numbers with Persian separators, and build Persian ordinals, with every cap and edge case spelled out.","g":"Numbers and text","h":[["Digits","digits"],["convert()","convert"],["Number formatting","separators"],["What it accepts","separator-input"],["Custom separators","custom-separators"],["Floats and exactness","float-precision"],["Size caps","limits"],["Ordinals","ordinal"]],"x":"Digits RtlyKit\\Number\\Digits converts between the three digit sets used in Iranian software: English (0-9), Persian (۰-۹, U+06F0 to U+06F9) and Arabic-Indic (٠-٩, U+0660 to U+0669). The class is pure string replacement: it never throws, never touches other characters, and does not change decimal or thousands separators. <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Number\\Digits; echo Digits::toPersian('Tel 0912 / 2024'), \"\\n\"; // Tel ۰۹۱۲ / ۲۰۲۴ echo Digits::toPersian(1234.5), \"\\n\"; // ۱۲۳۴.۵ echo Digits::toArabic(2026), \"\\n\"; // ٢٠٢٦ echo Digits::toEnglish('۱۲۳ ٤٥٦ abc'), \"\\n\"; // 123 456 abc echo Digits::arabicToPersian('٠١٢ 345'), \"\\n\"; // ۰۱۲ 345 Method Input What it does toPersian($value) string, int, float English digits become Persian. Persian and Arabic-Indic digits that are already in a string are left as they are toArabic($value) string, int, float English digits become Arabic-Indic toEnglish($value) string Persian and Arabic-Indic digits both become English arabicToPersian($value) string Only Arabic-Indic digits become Persian; English digits stay convert($value, $to) string Any digit set to English first, then to the target convert() convert() normalizes to English first and then renders the target set. The target name is case-insensitive: persian, fa and farsi give Persian digits; arabic and ar give Arabic-Indic digits; anything else, including a typo, gives English. echo Digits::convert('۱۲۳', 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // ١٢٣ echo Digits::convert('123', 'fa'), \"\\n\"; // ۱۲۳ echo Digits::convert('٣٤٥', 'FARSI'), \"\\n\"; // ۳۴۵ echo Digits::convert('۱۲۳', 'x'), \"\\n\"; // 123 Numbers as input. toPersian() and toArabic() accept int and float and convert them with a plain (string) cast, so the decimal separator stays a Latin dot (۱۲۳۴.۵) and there are no thousands separators. For human-facing numbers use Format::withSeparator() below. The helpers to_persian_digits(), to_english_digits(), to_persian() and to_english() wrap these methods; see Helpers and globals. The validators use Digits::toEnglish() for their normalization, so users may type in any digit set; see Normalization. Number formatting Format::withSeparator() formats a number with thousands separators and Persian digits. By default it uses the Arabic thousands separator ٬ (U+066C) and decimal separator ٫ (U+066B), the characters Persian typography prescribes. It never rounds. use RtlyKit\\Number\\Format; echo Format::withSeparator(1234567), \"\\n\"; // ۱٬۲۳۴٬۵۶۷ echo Format::withSeparator('1,234,567.5'), \"\\n\"; // ۱٬۲۳۴٬۵۶۷٫۵ echo Format::withSeparator('۱٬۲۳۴٫۵'), \"\\n\"; // ۱٬۲۳۴٫۵ echo Format::withSeparator('0001234'), \"\\n\"; // ۱٬۲۳۴ echo Format::withSeparator('+12345'), \"\\n\"; // ۱۲٬۳۴۵ echo Format::withSeparator(1.0), \"\\n\"; // ۱ What it accepts An int, a finite float, or a numeric string with optional sign, digits in any of the three sets, and an optional fraction. In strings, these are discarded as grouping marks: ,, ٬ (U+066C), regular spaces and the no-break space. The Arabic decimal separator ٫ (U+066B) is read as the decimal point. A plain . is the decimal point too. Leading zeros of the integer part are dropped, a leading + is dropped, and a leading - is kept. Anything that is not a plain decimal after this cleanup throws InvalidNumberException: 'abc', the empty string, scientific notation in a string ('1e5') and NaN or INF. The exception carries a stable error code (invalid_number, non_finite_number, input_too_long) that you can read with getErrorCode(); see Error handling. Custom separators The second and third arguments replace the thousands and decimal separators. The digits are still converted to Persian: echo Format::withSeparator(1234567, ','), \"\\n\"; // ۱,۲۳۴,۵۶۷ echo Format::withSeparator(1234567.5, ',', '.'), \"\\n\"; // ۱,۲۳۴,۵۶۷.۵ For English digits, convert the result back with Digits::toEnglish(). Floats and exactness A float is expanded to 15 decimal places and trailing zeros are trimmed, so binary representation noise can show up. If you need an exact decimal, pass a string: echo Format::withSeparator(0.1 + 0.2), \"\\n\"; // ۰٫۳ echo Format::withSeparator(-1234567.891), \"\\n\"; // -۱٬۲۳۴٬۵۶۷٫۸۹۱۰۰۰۰۰۰۰۶۱۴۶۷ echo Format::withSeparator('-1234567.891'), \"\\n\"; // -۱٬۲۳۴٬۵۶۷٫۸۹۱ Money and large values. Keep amounts as strings or integers (rials) end to end. Floats cannot represent most decimals exactly, and the formatter reproduces whatever digits the float actually holds. Size caps Cap Applies to Error code 4096 bytes a raw string input (Persian and Arabic digits are two bytes each) input_too_long 1000 characters the normalized number: integer and fraction digits together input_too_long try { Format::withSeparator(str_repeat('9', 1001)); } catch (\\RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidNumberException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(), ' [', $e->getErrorCode()->value, \"]\\n\"; // Number exceeds the 1000 character limit. [input_too_long] } Exactly 1000 digits are formatted normally. All the caps in one table are in Limits. The helper format_number() calls Format::withSeparator() with the defaults. Ordinals Format::ordinal() returns the Persian ordinal word for a non-negative integer. It builds the cardinal words with NumberToWords (see Number to words) and then applies the suffix rules: 1 is the irregular «اول». A cardinal ending in «سه» becomes «سوم»; this also covers 23, 33 and so on. A cardinal ending in «ی» (as in «سی») takes a half-space and «ام». Everything else just adds «م». echo Format::ordinal(1), \"\\n\"; // اول echo Format::ordinal(2), \"\\n\"; // دوم echo Format::ordinal(3), \"\\n\"; // سوم echo Format::ordinal(21), \"\\n\"; // بیست و یکم echo Format::ordinal(23), \"\\n\"; // بیست و سوم echo Format::ordinal(30), \"\\n\"; // سی‌ام echo Format::ordinal(33), \"\\n\"; // سی و سوم echo Format::ordinal(100), \"\\n\"; // صدم echo Format::ordinal(1000), \"\\n\"; // یک هزارم echo Format::ordinal(0), \"\\n\"; // صفرم echo Format::ordinal(3.0), \"\\n\"; // سوم Integral floats like 3.0 are accepted. Negative numbers, fractions (2.5) and non-finite floats throw"},{"t":"Number to words","u":"number-words.html","d":"Spell integers out in Persian (up to 10^21) and Modern Standard Arabic (below 10^9), and read Persian number words back into numbers with fromWords().","g":"Numbers and text","h":[["Converting numbers to words","convert"],["Accepted input","accepted-input"],["Errors","errors"],["Arabic","arabic"],["From words back to a number","from-words"],["How words are read","from-words-rules"]],"x":"Converting numbers to words NumberToWords::convert() spells an integer out as words. Persian (fa) is the default and covers every integer below 1021. <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Number\\NumberToWords; use function RtlyKit\\number_to_words; echo NumberToWords::convert(0), \"\\n\"; // صفر echo NumberToWords::convert(21), \"\\n\"; // بیست و یک echo NumberToWords::convert(115), \"\\n\"; // صد و پانزده echo NumberToWords::convert(1001), \"\\n\"; // یک هزار و یک echo NumberToWords::convert(12345), \"\\n\"; // دوازده هزار و سیصد و چهل و پنج echo NumberToWords::convert(-45), \"\\n\"; // منفی چهل و پنج echo number_to_words(2000000000), \"\\n\"; // دو میلیارد echo NumberToWords::convert(1234567), \"\\n\"; // یک میلیون و دویست و سی و چهار هزار و پانصد و شصت و هفت Words are joined with « و » (and) between every part, and each scale word follows its group: «هزار» (103), «میلیون» (106), «میلیارد» (109), «تریلیون» (1012), «کوادریلیون» (1015) and «کوینتیلیون» (1018). A group equal to 1 keeps its «یک» (1000 is «یک هزار»; to read a bare «هزار» use fromWords(), below). Zero groups are skipped. Accepted input The parameter is typed int|float|string: int: the whole PHP range works, including PHP_INT_MAX: «نه کوینتیلیون و دویست و بیست و سه کوادریلیون ...». float: must be finite and integral. 3.0 gives «سه»; 1.5 throws instead of being silently truncated. string: an optional sign and digits in any digit set. Commas, ٬ and spaces are removed, so a number copied from a form works. Strings can be longer than a PHP int, up to 21 digits after leading zeros are dropped. echo NumberToWords::convert('۱۲۳٬۴۵۶'), \"\\n\"; // صد و بیست و سه هزار و چهارصد و پنجاه و شش echo NumberToWords::convert('1,000,000'), \"\\n\"; // یک میلیون echo NumberToWords::convert('+7'), \"\\n\"; // هفت echo NumberToWords::convert('-0'), \"\\n\"; // صفر echo NumberToWords::convert('999999999999999999999'), \"\\n\"; // نهصد و نود و نه کوینتیلیون و نهصد و نود و نه کوادریلیون ... و نهصد و نود و نه Errors Invalid input throws InvalidNumberException (a subclass of RtlyKitException). Read the stable code with getErrorCode(): Input Error code 1.5, '12a', '' invalid_number NAN, INF non_finite_number A string longer than 4096 bytes input_too_long 1e21, '1000000000000000000000' (22 digits) number_too_large try { NumberToWords::convert('1000000000000000000000'); } catch (\\RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidNumberException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(), ' [', $e->getErrorCode()->value, \"]\\n\"; // Number is too large (limit is 10^21 - 1). [number_too_large] } See Error handling for how to catch every library exception with one type. Arabic Pass 'ar' as the second argument for Modern Standard Arabic. The locale is matched on its first two letters, case-insensitively, so 'ar_SA' and 'AR' work and so do 'fa_IR' for Persian. echo NumberToWords::convert(11, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // أحد عشر echo NumberToWords::convert(21, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // واحد وعشرون echo NumberToWords::convert(200, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // مئتان echo NumberToWords::convert(3000, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // ثلاثة آلاف echo NumberToWords::convert(2000000, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // مليونان echo NumberToWords::convert(1002003, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // مليون وألفان وثلاثة echo NumberToWords::convert(-7, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // سالب سبعة The Arabic output follows these rules, and its limits are real: Range: the absolute value must be below 109 (up to 999,999,999). A larger number throws number_too_large. The converter is tested for 0 to 999,999,999 and for negatives. Masculine counting form only. There is no gender agreement with the counted noun, no case endings and no tanwin. Compound groups are plain. A group such as 102 in 102,000 uses the ordinary compound form («مئة واثنان ألف») without the special construct-state rules some style guides require. Negative numbers are prefixed with «سالب». Known limitation. Treat the Arabic output as a readable spelled-out number, not as legal or typeset-quality text for a cheque. If the counted noun's gender or grammatical case matters, review the phrase by hand. An unsupported locale throws UnsupportedLocaleException with the code unsupported_locale: try { NumberToWords::convert(5, 'en'); } catch (\\RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\UnsupportedLocaleException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(), \"\\n\"; // Unsupported locale 'en' (use 'fa' or 'ar'). } From words back to a number NumberToWords::fromWords() is the inverse of the Persian conversion for the forms convert() produces. It returns an int when the value fits in a PHP int and a digit string otherwise. var_dump(NumberToWords::fromWords('بیست و سه')); // int(23) var_dump(NumberToWords::fromWords('منفی چهل و دو')); // int(-42) var_dump(NumberToWords::fromWords('هزار و یک')); // int(1001) var_dump(NumberToWords::fromWords('دو میلیون و سیصد هزار و پنج')); // int(2300005) // Round trip across the whole range: var_dump(NumberToWords::fromWords(NumberToWords::convert(PHP_INT_MAX))); // int(9223372036854775807) var_dump(NumberToWords::fromWords(NumberToWords::convert('999999999999999999999'))); // string(21) \"999999999999999999999\" How words are read Input is normalized first: Arabic letter variants become Persian («ك» to «ک», «ي» to «ی»), a ZWNJ counts as a space, and extra whitespace is ignored. A leading «منفی» makes the result negative; «منفی صفر» is 0. A scale word without a preceding number means one, so «هزار» is 1000. Scales must be in descending order: «هزار هزار» or «هزار میلیون» throw. No group may exceed 999. The word «و» is skipped wherever it appears. try { NumberToWords::fromWords('یکصد'); } catch (\\RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidNumberException $e) { echo $e->getMessage(), ' [', $e->getErrorCode()->value, \"]\\n\"; // Unknown number word 'یکصد'. [invalid_number_words] } Errors carry the code invalid_number_words (unknown word, ill-formed sequence, scales out of order, empty text) or input_too_long for text over 4096 bytes. Lenient reader. fromWords() adds up the values inside each group without checking natural word order, so unusual phrases such as «دو صد» (102) or a stray trailing «و» are accepted. It is a reader"},{"t":"Text tools","u":"text-tools.html","d":"Normalize Persian and Arabic text, repair half-spaces, detect language and direction, and build URL-safe slugs, with the exact behavior and edge cases of each tool.","g":"Numbers and text","h":[["Normalizer","normalizer"],["normalize()","normalize"],["fixHalfSpace()","half-space"],["clean()","clean"],["Detector","detector"],["isPersian() and isArabic()","persian-arabic"],["Direction and RTL","direction"],["isRtlLocale()","rtl-locale"],["Slugify","slugify"],["The separator","slug-separator"]],"x":"Normalizer RtlyKit\\Text\\Normalizer makes Persian text comparable and storable. User input mixes Arabic and Persian letterforms (an Arabic keyboard types «ي» and «ك»), diacritics, tatweel and stray whitespace; the same word then looks identical on screen but fails a string comparison or a database search. normalize() Normalizer::normalize($text, $removeDiacritics = true) does the following: Arabic letters become their Persian counterparts: «ك» to «ک», «ي» «ى» «ے» to «ی», «ة» «ۀ» to «ه», «ؤ» to «و», «إ» «أ» «ٱ» to «ا». Arabic-Indic digits (٠-٩) become Persian digits (۰-۹). English digits are left alone. Tatweel (the stretching character U+0640) is removed. Harakat, the short-vowel marks (U+064B to U+065F and U+0670), are removed unless you pass false as the second argument. Runs of whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) collapse to a single space, and the ends are trimmed. <?php require 'vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Text\\Normalizer; use function RtlyKit\\normalize_text; echo Normalizer::normalize(\"علي كتاب ١٢٣ 456\"), \"\\n\"; // علی کتاب ۱۲۳ 456 echo Normalizer::normalize(\"كــتاب\"), \"\\n\"; // کتاب echo Normalizer::normalize(\" سلام \\n\\t دنیا \"), \"\\n\"; // سلام دنیا echo Normalizer::normalize(\"أحمد إبراهيم مؤمن ة\"), \"\\n\"; // احمد ابراهیم مومن ه echo Normalizer::normalize(\"ثَبْت ١٢٣ 456\"), \"\\n\"; // ثبت ۱۲۳ 456 echo Normalizer::normalize(\"ثَبْت\", false), \"\\n\"; // ثَبْت (diacritics kept) echo normalize_text('كتاب'), \"\\n\"; // کتاب What it leaves alone. Standalone hamza «ء» and «ئ» are intentionally untouched, because they are meaningful in Persian and Arabic words. The half-space (ZWNJ) is preserved: «می‌خواهم» comes out byte-identical. English letters are unchanged. The helper normalize_text() calls Normalizer::normalize() with the defaults. fixHalfSpace() The half-space (ZWNJ, U+200C) is what makes «می‌خواهم» look right. Copy and paste often leaves several of them in a row, or one hanging next to a space or at the end of the text. fixHalfSpace() cleans that up: Soft hyphens (U+00AD) are removed. A run of ZWNJs collapses to one. A ZWNJ directly before or after whitespace is removed. ZWNJs at the very start and end of the text are removed. $a = \"می\\u{200C}\\u{200C}\\u{200C}خواهم\"; echo mb_strlen($a), ' ', mb_strlen(Normalizer::fixHalfSpace($a)), \"\\n\"; // 10 8 $b = \"\\u{200C}سلام \\u{200C}دنیا\\u{200C}\"; echo mb_strlen($b), ' ', mb_strlen(Normalizer::fixHalfSpace($b)), \"\\n\"; // 12 9 echo Normalizer::fixHalfSpace($b), \"\\n\"; // سلام دنیا It does not insert missing half-spaces and does not collapse ordinary spaces. clean() Normalizer::clean() is the all-in-one version for storage and search: normalize(), then fixHalfSpace(), then removal of the invisible characters zero-width space (U+200B), zero-width joiner (U+200D) and the byte order mark (U+FEFF). ZWNJ is the only zero-width character kept. $c = \"كتاب\\u{200B}ي ١٢٣\"; echo Normalizer::clean($c), \"\\n\"; // کتابی ۱۲۳ Applying normalize() alone to the same text would leave the invisible zero-width space inside the word, which is why clean() exists. Use it before storing or indexing user-supplied text. Detector RtlyKit\\Text\\Detector answers questions about script, language and direction. All methods are static and never throw. isPersian() and isArabic() Both are heuristics for Arabic-script text. Persian is recognised by letters that exist only in Persian (پ چ ژ گ, the Persian forms ک and ی, and Persian digits). Arabic is recognised by ي ك ى ة and Arabic-Indic digits. Letters shared by both languages, including «ه» and the hamza forms, are not counted. The text is Persian when Persian-only letters are at least as frequent as Arabic-only letters, so ties and text with no distinguishing letters count as Persian. use RtlyKit\\Text\\Detector; var_dump(Detector::isPersian('سلام')); // bool(true), no distinguishing letters, Persian by default var_dump(Detector::isPersian('گچپژ')); // bool(true) var_dump(Detector::isArabic('مرحبا بكم')); // bool(true) var_dump(Detector::isArabic('كتاب ي')); // bool(true) var_dump(Detector::isPersian('hello')); // bool(false), no Arabic script at all var_dump(Detector::isPersian('۱۲۳')); // bool(true), Persian digits var_dump(Detector::isArabic('٣٤٥')); // bool(true), Arabic-Indic digits var_dump(Detector::isPersian('')); // bool(false) Known limitation. This is a letter-frequency heuristic, not a language identifier. Very short text, names and Arabic words written in Persian are ambiguous. Text mixing Arabic and Persian can go either way. Do not use it for decisions that must be right; use it to choose a sensible default. Direction and RTL containsRtl($text): whether the text contains any character from a right-to-left script (Hebrew, Arabic and its supplements, Syriac, Thaana, N'Ko and others, Arabic presentation forms) or the RLM mark. direction($text): 'rtl' or 'ltr' from the first strong letter, following the Unicode bidirectional rule. Digits, punctuation and spaces are neutral. Text with no letters returns 'ltr'. isHebrew($text): whether the text contains Hebrew letters. var_dump(Detector::containsRtl('abc')); // bool(false) echo Detector::direction('سلام hello'), \"\\n\"; // rtl echo Detector::direction('Hello مرحبا'), \"\\n\"; // ltr (the first letter is Latin) echo Detector::direction('۱۲۳'), \"\\n\"; // ltr (digits are neutral, there is no letter) var_dump(Detector::isHebrew('שלום')); // bool(true) The helpers contains_rtl() and text_direction() wrap containsRtl() and direction(). Use direction() for an HTML dir attribute on user-generated text. isRtlLocale() isRtlLocale($locale) says whether a locale tag is written right-to-left. It splits on -, _, . and @, ignores case and surrounding spaces, and returns true when the language is a right-to-left one (fa, ar, he, ur, ps, ug, dv, ckb, azb and others) or when a script subtag such as Arab or Hebr is present. var_dump(Detector::isRtlLocale('fa_IR')); // bool(true) var_dump(Detector::isRtlLocale('ar-SA')); // bool(true) var_dump(Detector::isRtlLocale('ckb-IQ')); // bool(true)"},{"t":"Iranian holidays","u":"holidays.html","d":"Look up official Iranian public holidays for any Jalali date, list a whole year, and find business days. Fixed solar holidays are exact; Islamic holidays are estimates.","g":"Holidays and prayer times","h":[["What it does","overview"],["Checking a single day","check"],["Listing a whole year","year"],["Weekends and business days","business"],["Invalid input","errors"],["Accuracy and limits","limits"]],"x":"What it does RtlyKit\\Holiday\\IranHolidays answers four questions about a Jalali date: is it an official public holiday, what is the holiday called, is it a weekend, and what is the next working day. All methods are static and the class is final. Holiday titles are returned in Persian, exactly as they appear on the official calendar. Two kinds of holiday are combined: Fixed solar holidays fall on the same Jalali date every year (Nowruz, 22 Bahman, and so on). They are exact. Islamic (lunar) holidays are derived from the Hijri calendar, so they move through the Jalali year by about 11 days annually. They are estimates, see Accuracy and limits. Checking a single day Every lookup accepts either a Jalali object or three integers (year, month, day). use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali; use RtlyKit\\Holiday\\IranHolidays; use function RtlyKit\\is_iran_holiday; var_dump(IranHolidays::isHoliday(1404, 11, 22)); // bool(true) var_dump(IranHolidays::getTitle(1404, 11, 22)); // string(38) \"پیروزی انقلاب اسلامی\" var_dump(IranHolidays::isHoliday(Jalali::create(1404, 1, 5))); // bool(false) var_dump(is_iran_holiday(1404, 1, 1)); // bool(true) (namespaced helper) getTitle() returns the first title or null. When two holidays coincide on one day, use getTitles(), which returns a list with the fixed holiday first and the Islamic one second: print_r(IranHolidays::getTitles(Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1))); // Array ( [0] => جشن نوروز [1] => شهادت امام علی ) The second entry appears because the Umm al-Qura calendar places 21 Ramadan 1446 on 1 Farvardin 1404. It is computed, not announced, which is exactly why Islamic holidays are estimates. See the limitation below. Listing a whole year Three methods return a year, each keyed by Y/m/d strings in ascending order: allFixed(int $year) returns only the fixed solar holidays, one title per date. all(int $year) returns fixed and Islamic holidays; titles falling on the same day are joined with / . allTitles(int $year) is the structured form: each value is a list<string>. foreach (IranHolidays::allFixed(1404) as $date => $title) { echo $date, ' ', $title, \"\\n\"; } // 1404/01/01 جشن نوروز // 1404/01/02 عید نوروز // 1404/01/03 عید نوروز // 1404/01/04 عید نوروز // 1404/01/12 روز جمهوری اسلامی // 1404/01/13 روز طبیعت // 1404/03/14 رحلت امام خمینی // 1404/03/15 قیام ۱۵ خرداد // 1404/11/22 پیروزی انقلاب اسلامی // 1404/12/29 ملی شدن صنعت نفت echo count(IranHolidays::all(1404)); // 27 (fixed + Islamic holidays, one entry per day) The Islamic part of 1404 is, for example, 1404/01/10 عید فطر, 1404/01/11 تعطیل عید فطر, 1404/03/16 عید قربان, 1404/03/24 عید غدیر خم, 1404/04/13 تاسوعای حسینی and 1404/04/14 عاشورای حسینی. Note that Eid al-Fitr also lands on 1404/12/29, because a Hijri year is shorter than a Jalali year and the same holiday can occur twice; all() then prints ملی شدن صنعت نفت / عید فطر on that day. Weekends and business days Three helpers take a Jalali object: isWeekend($date) is true only on Friday. isBusinessDay($date) is true when the day is neither a weekend nor a holiday. nextBusinessDay($date) returns the first business day strictly after the given date. foreach ([Jalali::create(1404, 11, 20), Jalali::create(1404, 11, 21), Jalali::create(1404, 11, 22)] as $d) { echo $d->toDateString(), ' weekend=', var_export(IranHolidays::isWeekend($d), true), ' business=', var_export(IranHolidays::isBusinessDay($d), true), ' next=', IranHolidays::nextBusinessDay($d)->toDateString(), \"\\n\"; } // 1404/11/20 weekend=false business=true next=1404/11/21 // 1404/11/21 weekend=false business=true next=1404/11/23 // 1404/11/22 weekend=false business=false next=1404/11/23 Note. Only Friday is treated as a weekend. Thursday is a business day here, and Thursday half-days, regional calendars and ad-hoc closures (for example weather or official announcements) are not modelled. If your organisation closes on Thursdays, filter that yourself after calling isBusinessDay(). Invalid input Bad input throws a subclass of RtlyKitException, never a raw PHP error. See Error handling. IranHolidays::isHoliday(1404, 13, 1); // InvalidDateException: Invalid Jalali date: 1404/13/1 IranHolidays::isHoliday(1404); // InvalidDateException: Month and day are required when the year is given as int. IranHolidays::all(99999); // InvalidDateException (year outside -620..9377) Accuracy and limits Known limitation. Fixed solar holidays are exact. Islamic holidays are computed from the Umm al-Qura Hijri calendar, but Iran fixes its religious dates by moon sighting. The announced Iranian day can therefore differ from the computed one by 1 to 2 days. Treat Islamic holidays as estimates and confirm them against the official announcement before you rely on them for payroll, deadlines or legal dates. Range. Islamic holidays are reported only inside the embedded Umm al-Qura table (Hijri years 1300 to 1500, 1882-11-12 to 2077-11-16). Outside it, isHoliday(), getTitles(), all() and allTitles() return the fixed solar holidays only, because tabular extrapolation is not reliable for holidays. Example: IranHolidays::getTitles(Jalali::create(1500, 1, 1)) returns just [\"جشن نوروز\"]. Not covered. Extra one-off holidays declared by the government, and the list of observances that are not days off. Source of the data is described in the accuracy and data page. Related: the Jalali calendar and the Hijri calendar."},{"t":"Prayer times","u":"prayer-times.html","d":"Compute Fajr, sunrise, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha for any location with six calculation methods, with an honest per-method verification status.","g":"Holidays and prayer times","h":[["Overview","overview"],["Quick start","quick"],["Creating a calculator","construct"],["Methods and angles","methods"],["Asr schools","asr"],["Makkah: Isha in Ramadan","makkah-isha"],["Elevation","elevation"],["Time zones and daylight saving","dst"],["The next prayer","next"],["High latitudes","high-latitude"],["Exceptions","errors"],["Verification status","verification"]],"x":"Overview RtlyKit\\Prayer\\PrayerTimes calculates the six daily times from astronomical equations, with no network access and no data file. The solar declination and equation of time come from the low-precision series in Jean Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms (the same equations as the NOAA Solar Calculator). The hour angle comes from spherical trigonometry, Asr from the shadow-length factor, and sunrise/sunset from an altitude of -0.833 degrees (refraction plus the sun's semi-diameter). The Fajr, Maghrib and Isha angles are the published conventions of each method, not values derived from other software. Results are strings in HH:MM format in the local time of the calculator's time zone, rounded to the nearest minute. Quick start use RtlyKit\\Prayer\\PrayerTimes; $tz = new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran'); $day = new DateTimeImmutable('2026-03-21 12:00', $tz); $times = PrayerTimes::forCity('tehran')->getTimes($day); echo json_encode($times); // {\"fajr\":\"04:43\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:35\",\"isha\":\"19:22\"} If you omit the date, getTimes() uses today in the calculator's time zone. The date you pass selects the local calendar day of that zone. There is also a namespaced shortcut that returns the same array for today: \\RtlyKit\\prayer_times('tehran', 'Tehran') (see helpers and globals). Creating a calculator There are two ways to build one. // 1) A built-in city: coordinates and time zone are filled in. $p = PrayerTimes::forCity('mecca', PrayerTimes::METHOD_MAKKAH); // 2) Your own coordinates. $p = new PrayerTimes( latitude: 35.6892, longitude: 51.3890, method: PrayerTimes::METHOD_TEHRAN, // default asrFactor: PrayerTimes::ASR_STANDARD, // default timezone: new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran'), // default: date_default_timezone_get() elevation: 0.0, // metres, default 0 ); forCity() knows these names (case-insensitive): tehran, mashhad, isfahan, shiraz, tabriz, qom, mecca, medina, riyadh, istanbul, cairo, dubai, baghdad, jakarta. Anything else throws, so for other cities use the constructor with your own coordinates. Methods and angles The method decides the sun's depression angle for Fajr and Isha (and, for Tehran, Maghrib). Use the class constants rather than strings. Constant Fajr Maghrib Isha METHOD_TEHRAN (Tehran, default) 17.7 degrees sun at 4.5 degrees below the horizon 14 degrees METHOD_MWL (MWL) 18 degrees sunset 17 degrees METHOD_ISNA (ISNA) 15 degrees sunset 15 degrees METHOD_EGYPT (Egypt) 19.5 degrees sunset 17.5 degrees METHOD_MAKKAH (Makkah) 18.5 degrees sunset Maghrib + 90 min (+120 min in Ramadan) METHOD_KARACHI (Karachi) 18 degrees sunset 18 degrees The same Tehran day computed with each method (only Fajr, Maghrib and Isha differ; sunrise, Dhuhr and Asr do not depend on the method): foreach (['Tehran', 'MWL', 'ISNA', 'Egypt', 'Makkah', 'Karachi'] as $m) { echo str_pad($m, 8), json_encode(PrayerTimes::forCity('tehran', $m)->getTimes($day)), \"\\n\"; } // Tehran {\"fajr\":\"04:43\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:35\",\"isha\":\"19:22\"} // MWL {\"fajr\":\"04:42\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:17\",\"isha\":\"19:37\"} // ISNA {\"fajr\":\"04:57\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:17\",\"isha\":\"19:27\"} // Egypt {\"fajr\":\"04:34\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:17\",\"isha\":\"19:40\"} // Makkah {\"fajr\":\"04:39\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:17\",\"isha\":\"19:47\"} // Karachi {\"fajr\":\"04:42\",\"sunrise\":\"06:07\",\"dhuhr\":\"12:12\",\"asr\":\"15:39\",\"maghrib\":\"18:17\",\"isha\":\"19:42\"} Dhuhr is solar noon (the sun crossing the meridian); no safety offset is added to any time. Asr schools Asr begins when an object's shadow equals its noon shadow plus a multiple of its height. ASR_STANDARD (1, one times the height) is used by the Shafi'i, Maliki, Hanbali and Ja'fari schools; ASR_HANAFI (2, twice the height) gives a later time. $hanafi = PrayerTimes::forCity('tehran', 'Tehran', PrayerTimes::ASR_HANAFI); echo $hanafi->getTimes($day)['asr']; // 16:32 (standard: 15:39) Makkah: Isha in Ramadan The Umm al-Qura convention sets Isha at 90 minutes after Maghrib, and at 120 minutes during Ramadan. The calculator picks 120 when the local calendar date falls in Ramadan of the package's own Umm al-Qura Hijri table (month 9), and 90 otherwise. The decision follows the civil date, not a sunset-based Islamic day. $mk = PrayerTimes::forCity('mecca', 'Makkah'); foreach (['2026-02-20', '2026-03-01', '2026-03-21'] as $s) { $t = $mk->getTimes(new DateTimeImmutable($s, new DateTimeZone('Asia/Riyadh'))); echo $s, ' maghrib ', $t['maghrib'], ' isha ', $t['isha'], \"\\n\"; } // 2026-02-20 maghrib 18:21 isha 20:21 (+120, Ramadan) // 2026-03-01 maghrib 18:25 isha 20:25 (+120, Ramadan) // 2026-03-21 maghrib 18:32 isha 20:02 (+90, after Ramadan) Elevation The elevation argument is the observer's height in metres. It lowers the sunrise/sunset altitude by the geometric dip 0.0347 * sqrt(h) degrees, so sunrise is earlier and sunset later on a mountain. Negative values are treated as 0. It affects sunrise, sunset and the times derived from sunset only. $a = new PrayerTimes(35.6892, 51.3890, 'Tehran', 1, $tz); $b = new PrayerTimes(35.6892, 51.3890, 'Tehran', 1, $tz, 1500.0); $d = new DateTimeImmutable('2026-06-21', $tz); echo $a->getTimes($d)['sunrise'], \"\\n\"; // 04:49 echo $b->getTimes($d)['sunrise'], \"\\n\"; // 04:41 For the Tehran method, Maghrib is defined by the 4.5-degree angle, not by sunset, so it does not change with elevation (19:45 in both cases above). Time zones and daylight saving Each time is converted using the zone's real UTC offset at that instant, so a daylight-saving change in the middle of the year is handled without any extra work. Berlin changes on 2026-03-29: $tzb = new DateTimeZone('Europe/Berlin'); $ber = new PrayerTimes(52.52, 13.405, 'MWL', 1, $tzb); foreach (['2026-03-28', '2026-03-29'] as $s) { echo $s, ' ', json_encode($ber->getTimes(new DateTimeImmutable($s, $tzb))), \"\\n\";"},{"t":"Laravel setup","u":"laravel-setup.html","d":"How RTLY-Kit plugs into Laravel: package auto-discovery, what the service provider registers, the Jalali facade, and the fa/en/ar validation messages you can override.","g":"Laravel","h":[["Install and auto-discovery","install"],["What the service provider does","provider"],["The Jalali facade","facade"],["Translations: fa, en, ar","translations"],["Overriding a message","override"],["Configuration","config"]],"x":"Install and auto-discovery Nothing is needed beyond Composer. The package declares its provider and facade alias in composer.json under extra.laravel, so Laravel's package discovery registers them on the next composer install or composer update. composer require enaxon/rtly-kit The integration uses the Laravel components illuminate/support, illuminate/translation, illuminate/validation and illuminate/database, which a Laravel application already has. The test suite runs against these components at version 11 and 12 (the development constraint is ^11.0||^12.0); the library itself needs only PHP 8.2 and works without Laravel. Note. The integration tests exercise the real container, translator, validation factory and Eloquent model classes, not a full application skeleton. The code samples on these pages were run the same way. If your application disables discovery for this package (\"dont-discover\": [\"enaxon/rtly-kit\"]), register the provider yourself. In Laravel 11 and later add it to bootstrap/providers.php: return [ App\\Providers\\AppServiceProvider::class, RtlyKit\\Laravel\\RtlyKitServiceProvider::class, ]; On older application layouts add the same class to the providers array in config/app.php. There is no configuration file to publish and no migration. What the service provider does RtlyKit\\Laravel\\RtlyKitServiceProvider has one job in each phase: register() binds a singleton under the container key rtly-kit.jalali, a RtlyKit\\Laravel\\JalaliFactory. This is the object behind the facade. boot() does three things: it installs the Carbon macros (see Carbon macros), loads the package translations under the rtly-kit namespace, and registers the validation rules when the container has a validation factory (see Validation and the cast). Note. The provider registers no global functions. A test boots it and asserts that none of the 27 short helper names exists afterwards. If you want the short global names, call \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register() yourself; see helpers and globals. If the container's validator is not an Illuminate\\Validation\\Factory (a custom replacement), the provider leaves it untouched and skips the rules instead of failing. The Jalali facade The alias Jalali (class RtlyKit\\Laravel\\Facades\\Jalali) forwards to the factory. It offers four methods that mirror the static constructors of RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali: use RtlyKit\\Laravel\\Facades\\Jalali; echo Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1)->format('Y/m/d l'); // 1404/01/01 جمعه echo Jalali::make('2025-03-21')->toDateString(); // 1404/01/01 echo get_class(Jalali::now()); // RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali // Jalali::today() is also available Every call returns an ordinary immutable Jalali object, so everything on the Jalali calendar page applies. The factory is a singleton, so a repeated app('rtly-kit.jalali') returns the same instance. The facade is optional. In classes you can import the class directly or use the namespaced helper \\RtlyKit\\jdate(); in tests there is nothing to fake because the factory is stateless. Translations: fa, en, ar The provider calls loadTranslationsFrom() with the rtly-kit namespace, so the messages live in rtly-kit::validation.* and follow the application locale. There are three complete sets, one line per rule: Key fa en national_code :attribute کد ملی معتبر نیست. The :attribute is not a valid Iranian national code. sheba :attribute شماره شبا معتبر نیست. The :attribute is not a valid Iranian Sheba (IBAN). bank_card :attribute شماره کارت بانکی معتبر نیست. The :attribute is not a valid Iranian bank card. iran_mobile, mobile :attribute شماره موبایل معتبر نیست. The :attribute is not a valid Iranian mobile number. postal_code :attribute کد پستی معتبر نیست. The :attribute is not a valid Iranian postal code. vehicle_plate :attribute پلاک خودرو معتبر نیست. The :attribute is not a valid Iranian vehicle plate. The Arabic file is resources/lang/ar/validation.php in the package, with the same keys. The same rule in three locales: // field named national_code, value '1234567890', rule national_code // locale fa: national code کد ملی معتبر نیست. // locale en: The national code is not a valid Iranian national code. // locale ar: national code ليس رقماً وطنياً إيرانياً صالحاً. (Laravel turns the field name national_code into the attribute label \"national code\"; give the field a proper label through the attributes translations to get a natural sentence.) If the active locale has no line, Laravel's normal fallback locale (app.fallback_locale) is used. If no translation can be found at all, the built-in English text is the last resort. Overriding a message You do not publish anything. Choose whichever fits, from the narrowest to the widest: Per validation call: pass custom messages to the validator or to $request->validate() in the usual way, for example key postal_code or zip.postal_code. A custom message always wins. Application-wide: add the rule's line to your own lang/fa/validation.php (the key is the rule name, for example 'sheba' => 'شبای :attribute اشتباه است.'). The application's validation.<rule> line is preferred over the package's. Replace the package texts: create lang/vendor/rtly-kit/fa/validation.php (or en, ar). Laravel's standard vendor-override mechanism loads it over the package file; you only need the keys you change. Verified output for options 2 and 3 with the locale fa: // lang/vendor/rtly-kit/fa/validation.php -> ['national_code' => 'کد ملی :attribute درست نیست.'] // lang/fa/validation.php -> ['sheba' => 'شبای :attribute اشتباه است.'] // custom message on the call -> ['postal_code' => 'کد پستی :attribute نادرست'] // // national_code: کد ملی x درست نیست. // sheba: شبای x اشتباه است. // postal_code: کد پستی x نادرست The field in these examples is named x, which is why x appears in the sentences. In a real form, set readable labels in the attributes array of your validation.php. Configuration There is no config file. Behaviour is fixed by design and by the locale of the application. The library reads the default time zone from PHP"},{"t":"Validation rules and Eloquent cast","u":"laravel-validation-and-cast.html","d":"Use the Iranian validation rules in Laravel forms, store Jalali dates in Eloquent models with JalaliCast, and show them in Blade.","g":"Laravel","h":[["Validation rules","rules"],["Using them","usage"],["What input the rules accept","input"],["The JalaliCast Eloquent cast","cast"],["Accepted values on assignment","cast-input"],["Errors","cast-errors"],["Serialising a model","cast-json"],["Form requests","request"],["Blade","blade"]],"x":"Validation rules Once the package is installed (Laravel setup), seven rules are available in any validator, form request or $request->validate() call. They wrap the validators documented on the validation pages. Rule Accepts Details national_code Iranian national code National code sheba Sheba / IBAN, with or without the IR prefix Sheba and bank card bank_card 16-digit bank card number (Luhn check) Sheba and bank card iran_mobile Iranian mobile number Mobile, postal code, plate mobile Short alias of iran_mobile identical behaviour postal_code 10-digit Iranian postal code Mobile, postal code, plate vehicle_plate Iranian vehicle plate, for example 12ب345-67 Mobile, postal code, plate Name clash. mobile is a generic word. If another package or your own code registers a rule named mobile, use iran_mobile everywhere to stay unambiguous. Using them use Illuminate\\Http\\Request; public function store(Request $request) { $data = $request->validate([ 'national_code' => 'required|national_code', 'mobile' => 'required|iran_mobile', 'card' => 'nullable|bank_card', 'iban' => 'nullable|sheba', 'postal' => 'required|postal_code', 'plate' => 'nullable|vehicle_plate', ]); } Run against bad data with the locale fa, a real validation run produced: // national_code = '1234567890', mobile = '0912', iban = 'IR000000000000000000000000' // card, postal and plate hold valid values and pass. // // national_code: national code کد ملی معتبر نیست. // mobile: mobile شماره موبایل معتبر نیست. // iban: iban شماره شبا معتبر نیست. The English text is \"The national code is not a valid Iranian national code.\" and the Arabic text is also available; see translations and overriding messages. What input the rules accept Strings. Persian and Arabic digits are accepted: ۰۰۱۳۵۴۲۴۱۹ passes national_code. Integers and floats are converted to a string first. Be careful with numbers that start with a zero: the JSON number 13542419 has lost the leading zeros of 0013542419 and fails. Send national codes, mobile numbers and postal codes as strings. Arrays, objects and booleans never reach the validator and simply fail the rule. Empty values. As with Laravel's own rules, an empty string and a missing key are skipped unless the field is required. An explicit null fails unless the field is nullable. Say what you mean with required or nullable. $f->make(['n' => '۰۰۱۳۵۴۲۴۱۹'], ['n' => 'national_code'])->passes(); // true $f->make(['n' => 13542419], ['n' => 'national_code'])->passes(); // false (leading zeros lost) $f->make(['n' => null], ['n' => 'nullable|national_code'])->passes(); // true $f->make(['n' => null], ['n' => 'national_code'])->passes(); // false $f->make(['n' => ''], ['n' => 'national_code'])->passes(); // true (skipped) $f->make(['n' => ''], ['n' => 'required|national_code'])->passes(); // false $f->make([], ['n' => 'national_code'])->passes(); // true (key missing) If you need the reason a value failed (wrong length, bad checksum, wrong type), call the standalone validators, which return a structured Result; see validators overview. The JalaliCast Eloquent cast RtlyKit\\Laravel\\Casts\\JalaliCast keeps the database column in Gregorian (Y-m-d H:i:s), as every tool expects, and exposes the attribute on the model as an immutable RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali object. use Illuminate\\Database\\Eloquent\\Model; use RtlyKit\\Laravel\\Casts\\JalaliCast; class Post extends Model { protected $guarded = []; protected $casts = [ 'published_at' => JalaliCast::class, ]; } $post = new Post(); $post->published_at = '1404/01/15 10:30'; echo $post->getAttributes()['published_at']; // 2025-04-04 10:30:00 (stored value) echo $post->published_at->format('Y/m/d H:i'); // 1404/01/15 10:30 echo get_class($post->published_at); // RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali echo $post->published_at->addDays(20)->format('Y/m/d'); // 1404/02/04 The column type stays datetime or timestamp; no migration change is needed. Accepted values on assignment Value assigned Stored (Gregorian) Jalali string '1404/01/15 10:30' 2025-04-04 10:30:00 Jalali date only, Persian digits and dashes '۱۴۰۴-۰۱-۱۵' 2025-04-04 00:00:00 Gregorian string '2025-04-04 08:00:00' 2025-04-04 08:00:00 Unix timestamp (int) 1743753600 2025-04-04 08:00:00 (with a UTC default time zone) Any DateTimeInterface (Carbon included) the same instant, Y-m-d H:i:s A Jalali object its Gregorian equivalent null or '' null A string is read as Jalali when its year is 1200 to 1599 and the separator is / or -, with an optional time HH:MM or HH:MM:SS after a space or T. Any other string goes through the normal date parser as Gregorian. Errors Bad input throws RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException when you assign it, never a raw PHP error: $post->published_at = '1404/13/01'; // Invalid Jalali date: 1404/13/1 $post->published_at = '1404/07/31'; // Invalid Jalali date: 1404/7/31 (Mehr has 30 days) $post->published_at = 'not a date'; // Unable to parse date: not a date $post->published_at = 1.5; // Cannot cast value for 'published_at' to a date. $post->published_at = ['x']; // Cannot cast value for 'published_at' to a date. Validate user input before it reaches the model (see the form-request example below), so a typo produces a form error, not an exception. Reading is lenient for null and the empty string (both give null), but an attribute holding unparseable text in the database throws InvalidDateException when read. Serialising a model Jalali, Hijri and Hebrew implement JsonSerializable: toJson() on a model with a JalaliCast attribute gives the same text as (string) $date, for example {\"published_at\":\"1404/01/01 10:00:00\"}. toArray() keeps the Jalali object; format it yourself if you need another pattern. Form requests There is no built-in rule for a Jalali date field, so validate the shape with Laravel's regex rule or a small custom rule, then let the cast do the conversion: public function rules(): array { return [ 'published_at' => ['required', 'regex:/^1[2-5]\\d\\d[\\/-]\\d{1,2}[\\/-]\\d{1,2}$/u'], ]; } A well-formed but impossible date such"},{"t":"Helpers and globals","u":"helpers-and-globals.html","d":"The 27 namespaced helper functions, how to import them, and the opt-in Globals::register() that adds short global names without ever overwriting yours.","g":"Reference","h":[["Why helpers live in a namespace","why"],["Using the helpers","import"],["All helpers","reference"],["Calendars","reference-calendar"],["Digits and numbers","reference-digits"],["Text","reference-text"],["Validation: yes/no","reference-bool"],["Validation: structured result","reference-result"],["Holidays and prayer times","reference-dates"],["Opt-in global names: Globals::register()","globals"],["Collision demo","collision"],["Which style should I use?","choosing"]],"x":"Why helpers live in a namespace Names such as is_mobile(), ordinal() or to_english() are short and generic. PHP has no way to \"override\" a global function: if two packages (or your application and a package) declare the same global name, the second declaration is a fatal error. A library that defines globals unconditionally can therefore break an application that was working before it was installed. RTLY-Kit avoids the problem by design: All 27 helpers are defined in the RtlyKit namespace. Composer loads them automatically, and they can never collide with anything. Short global names are opt-in through \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(), which only defines names that are still free. The Laravel service provider does not register globals either. See Laravel setup. Using the helpers Import the ones you need with use function, then call them by their short name. Or call them fully qualified: use function RtlyKit\\jdate; use function RtlyKit\\is_national_code; echo jdate('2025-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'); // 1404/01/01 var_dump(is_national_code('0013542419')); // bool(true) echo \\RtlyKit\\to_persian_digits(1404); // ۱۴۰۴ // Several at once (PHP group use): use function RtlyKit\\{hdate, hebrew_date, number_to_words}; echo hdate('2025-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'); // 1446/09/21 echo hebrew_date('2025-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'); // 5785/06/21 echo number_to_words(1404); // یک هزار و چهارصد و چهار The helpers are thin wrappers: each calls the matching class, so you can always use the class directly when you need more control (for example Jalali::create() with a time zone). All helpers Parameter and return types are exactly those of the source. mixed inputs never throw: invalid input simply gives false or an invalid Result. Calendars Helper Signature Delegates to jdate jdate(DateTimeInterface|string|int|null $time = null, ?DateTimeZone $timezone = null): Jalali Jalali::make(), see Jalali hdate hdate(DateTimeInterface|string|int|null $time = null, ?DateTimeZone $timezone = null): Hijri Hijri::make(), see Hijri hebrew_date hebrew_date(DateTimeInterface|string|int|null $time = null, ?DateTimeZone $timezone = null): Hebrew Hebrew::make(), see Hebrew Digits and numbers Helper Signature Example to_persian_digits (string|int|float $value): string to_persian_digits(1404) gives ۱۴۰۴ to_english_digits (string $value): string to_english_digits('۱۴۰۴') gives 1404 to_persian (string|int|float $value): string short alias of to_persian_digits; to_persian('12.5') gives ۱۲.۵ to_english (string $value): string short alias of to_english_digits; to_english('٣٤') gives 34 number_to_words (int|float|string $number, string $locale = 'fa'): string number_to_words(25, 'ar') gives خمسة وعشرون format_number (int|float|string $number): string format_number(1234567) gives ۱٬۲۳۴٬۵۶۷ ordinal (int|float $number): string ordinal(3) gives سوم See digits and formatting and number words. Text Helper Signature Example normalize_text (string $text): string normalize_text('كتاب ي') gives کتاب ی (Arabic kaf and yeh become Persian) contains_rtl (string $text): bool contains_rtl('abc سلام') gives true text_direction (string $text): string text_direction('سلام') gives rtl, text_direction('hello') gives ltr See text tools. Validation: yes/no Each returns bool and accepts mixed. Helper Signature Example is_national_code (mixed $value): bool is_national_code('0013542419') gives true is_sheba (mixed $value): bool is_sheba('IR062960000000100324200001') gives true is_bank_card (mixed $value): bool is_bank_card('6037997535328737') gives true is_mobile (mixed $value): bool is_mobile('09123456789') gives true is_postal_code (mixed $value): bool is_postal_code('1676543210') gives true is_vehicle_plate (mixed $value): bool is_vehicle_plate('12ب345-67') gives true Validation: structured result Each returns a RtlyKit\\Validation\\Result with the methods valid(), invalid(), isValid(), errors() and details(). See validators overview. Helper Signature validate_national_code (mixed $value): Result validate_sheba (mixed $value): Result validate_bank_card (mixed $value): Result validate_mobile (mixed $value): Result validate_postal_code (mixed $value): Result validate_vehicle_plate (mixed $value): Result $r = \\RtlyKit\\validate_national_code('1234567890'); var_dump($r->isValid()); // bool(false) Holidays and prayer times Helper Signature Notes is_iran_holiday (Jalali|int $year, ?int $month = null, ?int $day = null): bool wraps IranHolidays::isHoliday(); is_iran_holiday(1404, 11, 22) gives true. See holidays. prayer_times (string $city = 'tehran', string $method = 'Tehran'): array times for today in the city's time zone, as {\"fajr\", \"sunrise\", \"dhuhr\", \"asr\", \"maghrib\", \"isha\"}. Unknown city or method throws InvalidPrayerConfigException. See prayer times. Together that is 3 + 7 + 3 + 6 + 6 + 2 = 27 helpers, the same list that Globals::NAMES holds. Opt-in global names: Globals::register() If you prefer the short global names (for example in a legacy code base, a template engine, or a script), call this once during bootstrap: $skipped = \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(); What it guarantees: Only free names are defined. A name is defined only if no function with that name exists. An existing function is never overwritten. It reports what it skipped. The return value is a list<string> of the names that were not defined because another function already uses them. An empty list means everything was registered. It never throws and never triggers a redeclaration error, whatever the situation. It is idempotent. Call it as many times as you like. A name that is RTLY-Kit's own earlier definition is not reported as skipped, so a second call returns the same list as the first. Namespaced functions always work, whether or not a global name was skipped. \\RtlyKit\\Globals::NAMES is the constant listing all 27 names. Collision demo The application already has its own is_mobile() (a user-agent check). The package does not break it, does not overwrite it, and tells you about it: require"},{"t":"Error handling","u":"error-handling.html","d":"Which calls throw, which never do, the exception hierarchy, the stable ErrorCode values, and how to catch and log failures safely.","g":"Reference","h":[["Two failure styles","two-styles"],["Exception hierarchy","hierarchy"],["Error codes","error-codes"],["Reading the code and the context","reading-codes"],["Catching patterns","catching"],["What never throws","never-throws"],["Input caps","caps"],["The calendar range rule","calendar-rule"],["A complete example","complete-example"]],"x":"Two failure styles Each function in RTLY-Kit uses exactly one of two styles, so you always know what to guard: Validators never throw. NationalCode, Sheba, BankCard, Mobile, PostalCode and VehiclePlate (and the is_* / validate_* helpers) accept any value and answer with false or an invalid Result. See Validators overview. Everything else throws a RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitException (or a subclass) for input it cannot accept. A TypeError, ValueError or PHP DateMalformed* exception never escapes for bad input; that is a library bug, please report it. Exception hierarchy \\InvalidArgumentException └─ RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitException implements RtlyKitThrowable ├─ InvalidDateException ├─ InvalidNumberException ├─ InvalidPrayerConfigException └─ UnsupportedLocaleException RtlyKitThrowable is a marker interface that declares getErrorCode(): ErrorCode and getContext(): array. Catch it (or RtlyKitException) to handle the whole library with one clause. Because RtlyKitException extends \\InvalidArgumentException, existing catch (\\InvalidArgumentException) and catch (\\LogicException) code keeps working. Exception Thrown for InvalidDateException impossible dates, years outside the supported range, blank or unparsable strings, absurd add*() / sub*() deltas, out-of-range timestamps, over-long format patterns InvalidNumberException non-numeric or fractional input, NaN / INF, oversized input, numbers that are too large, unknown number words InvalidPrayerConfigException unknown prayer method, unknown city, invalid Asr factor UnsupportedLocaleException a locale the function does not support (for example number_to_words(5, 'de')) RtlyKitException itself other bad arguments, such as an invalid or over-long Slugify::make() separator, or a missing bundled data table Error codes Exception messages are meant for humans and may be reworded in any release. The error code is the stable part: it is an ErrorCode backed string enum, and its case names and values are public API (see API stability). Case Value Meaning InvalidArgument invalid_argument generic fallback for an invalid argument with no more specific code InvalidDate invalid_date malformed, impossible or unparsable date or time (month 13, 30 Esfand in a non-leap year, 'not a date', a blank string) DateOutOfRange date_out_of_range a year, date, timestamp or add*() / sub*() delta outside the supported range (see Limits) InvalidNumber invalid_number the value cannot be read as a (whole) number NumberTooLarge number_too_large number larger than the supported magnitude NonFiniteNumber non_finite_number NaN or INF where a finite number is required InvalidNumberWords invalid_number_words unknown or ill-formed number words in NumberToWords::fromWords() InputTooLong input_too_long string input over a documented size cap InvalidPrayerConfig invalid_prayer_config unknown city, calculation method or Asr factor UnsupportedLocale unsupported_locale a locale other than a supported one DataUnavailable data_unavailable a bundled data table is missing or corrupt (a packaging problem, not a user error) Note. The strings returned by Result::errors() (invalid_format, invalid_checksum, invalid_type, ...) are plain strings of the validators, not ErrorCode cases. The enum is for exceptions only. Reading the code and the context Every library exception has getErrorCode() and getContext(). The context is an array of machine-readable facts about the failure (a limit, the offending locale, the name of the argument). It never contains secrets and may be empty. This script feeds five bad inputs to number_to_words(): <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; use function RtlyKit\\number_to_words; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitThrowable; foreach ([1.5, NAN, 'abc', str_repeat('9', 22), str_repeat('1', 5000)] as $input) { try { number_to_words($input); } catch (RtlyKitThrowable $e) { echo (new ReflectionClass($e))->getShortName(), ' ', $e->getErrorCode()->value, ' ', json_encode($e->getContext()), \"\\n\"; } } // InvalidNumberException invalid_number [] // InvalidNumberException non_finite_number [] // InvalidNumberException invalid_number [] // InvalidNumberException number_too_large {\"limit\":\"10^21 - 1\"} // InvalidNumberException input_too_long {\"limit\":4096} Catching patterns Branch on the code, not on the message. Use match with a default arm, because new cases may be added in a minor release. <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali; use RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\{ErrorCode, InvalidDateException, RtlyKitThrowable}; function category(RtlyKitThrowable $e): string { return match ($e->getErrorCode()) { ErrorCode::InvalidDate, ErrorCode::DateOutOfRange => 'date', ErrorCode::InputTooLong => 'too-long', default => 'other', }; } try { Jalali::create(1404, 13, 1); // there is no month 13 } catch (RtlyKitThrowable $e) { echo category($e), \"\\n\"; // date } $e = InvalidDateException::because(ErrorCode::DateOutOfRange, 'Year is out of range', ['year' => 99999]); echo $e->getErrorCode()->value, ' ', json_encode($e->getContext()), \"\\n\"; // date_out_of_range {\"year\":99999} try { Jalali::create(1404, 13, 1); } catch (\\InvalidArgumentException $e) { // old-style catch still works echo get_class($e), ' ', $e instanceof RtlyKitThrowable ? 'is RtlyKitThrowable' : '', \"\\n\"; // RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\InvalidDateException is RtlyKitThrowable } RtlyKitException::because(ErrorCode, string $message, array $context = [], ?Throwable $previous = null) is the named constructor the library itself uses to raise an exception with a specific code. You can use it for your own failures too, but code you add this way is your responsibility, not part of the library's contract. When logging, write the code and the context rather than the message text: catch (RtlyKitThrowable $e) { error_log($e->getErrorCode()->value.' '.json_encode($e->getContext())); } What never throws The validators accept any PHP value. They never raise an exception for bad input, whatever its type or size: <?php require"},{"t":"API stability","u":"api-stability.html","d":"What you can rely on, what is internal, how versions are numbered before and after 1.0, and which PHP, Laravel and Carbon versions are supported.","g":"Reference","h":[["Public API","public-api"],["Internal (no promise)","internal"],["Versioning policy","versioning"],["Supported platforms","platforms"]],"x":"Public API Everything in this table is covered by the compatibility promise. Area Public symbols Calendars RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali, Hijri, Hebrew, HijriVariant; the MIN_YEAR / MAX_YEAR constants; RtlyKit\\Contracts\\CalendarDate Validation RtlyKit\\Contracts\\Validator; RtlyKit\\Validation\\NationalCode, Sheba, BankCard, Mobile, PostalCode, VehiclePlate, Result, and the error-code strings they return Numbers and text RtlyKit\\Number\\Digits, Format, NumberToWords; RtlyKit\\Text\\Normalizer, Detector, Slugify Holidays, prayer times RtlyKit\\Holiday\\IranHolidays, RtlyKit\\Prayer\\PrayerTimes (its METHOD_* / ASR_* constants and public methods) Errors RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitThrowable, RtlyKitException, InvalidDateException, InvalidNumberException, InvalidPrayerConfigException, UnsupportedLocaleException, and ErrorCode (case names and values) Helpers the 27 namespaced functions in RtlyKit\\ (RtlyKit\\jdate() and so on) and RtlyKit\\Globals::register() / Globals::NAMES Laravel RtlyKit\\Laravel\\RtlyKitServiceProvider, Facades\\Jalali, JalaliFactory, Casts\\JalaliCast, the validation rule names (national_code, sheba, bank_card, iran_mobile, mobile, postal_code, vehicle_plate) and the translation keys under the rtly-kit namespace Carbon the macro names toJalali, jformat, toHijri, toHebrew, createFromJalali, createFromHijri, createFromHebrew The documented behaviour of these symbols is part of the contract: accepted inputs, the exception class and ErrorCode thrown, return types, the year ranges and the input caps. The wording of exception and validation messages is not; match on getErrorCode() or Result::errors() instead (see Error handling). Internal (no promise) These carry an @internal docblock or are implementation detail. They can change or disappear in any release, including a patch release. Do not call, extend or depend on them. RtlyKit\\Calendar\\CalendarLimits RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jdn RtlyKit\\Calendar\\CalendarDateTrait RtlyKit\\Calendar\\UmmAlQuraTable RtlyKit\\Validation\\Input RtlyKit\\Validation\\DataTables RtlyKit\\Support\\CarbonMacros and RtlyKit\\Support\\AutoLoader (registration plumbing; use the macros, not these classes) the files in resources/data/ (read the data through the public classes only) src/functions-global.php (reach it through Globals::register()) private and protected members of every class, and the constructors of final classes unless documented Warning. Reading resources/data/*.php directly, or calling a class listed above, is unsupported. Earlier pre-release builds kept the reference tables in classes of their own; those were never API, and the data now lives in resources/data/ (see Upgrade). Also not covered: the exact contents of the embedded reference tables (bank BINs, Sheba bank codes, operator prefixes, national-code prefixes, Umm al-Qura months). They are corrected when better sources appear, and a correction is not a breaking change. See Accuracy and data. Versioning policy RTLY-Kit follows Semantic Versioning. From 1.0.0: breaking changes to the public API only in a major release. Minor releases add features and may add enum cases, methods and error codes. Patch releases fix bugs only. Before 1.0.0 (now): a minor release (0.x) may contain breaking changes. Each one is listed under \"Changed\" in the changelog, with migration steps on the Upgrade page. Patch releases (0.x.y) do not break the public API. Pin a minor range in composer.json (for example ~0.1.0) and read the upgrade notes before moving to the next one. A bug fix that makes a function return the correct answer is a fix, not a breaking change, even if the output changes. Example: Makkah-method Isha during Ramadan moved 30 minutes later when it was corrected. Adding a case to ErrorCode, or a key to a Result::details() array, is not breaking. Write match over ErrorCode with a default arm. The interfaces (CalendarDate, Validator, RtlyKitThrowable) are for consuming. The library may add methods to them in a minor release before 1.0.0 and in a major release afterwards, so do not implement them yourself. Deprecations are announced in the changelog and in @deprecated tags, and are kept for at least one minor release after 1.0.0. Supported platforms Supported PHP 8.2, 8.3 and 8.4 (CI runs all three); ext-mbstring is required Laravel (illuminate/*) 11 and 12 Carbon 3 (nesbot/carbon ^3.0) The only required dependency is PHP itself (plus ext-mbstring). Carbon and Laravel are optional integrations. Dropping support for a PHP, Laravel or Carbon version is announced in the changelog and happens in a minor release before 1.0.0 and in a major release afterwards."},{"t":"Accuracy and data","u":"accuracy-and-data.html","d":"Where every embedded data table comes from, what has been verified against what, and what has not been verified. Read it before you rely on the results in production.","g":"Reference","h":[["Our principle","principle"],["The two-source rule","two-source-rule"],["The data tables","tables"],["Verification status per feature","status"],["Prayer times: status per method","prayer-status"]],"x":"Our principle RTLY-Kit has no runtime dependency, so its reference data is embedded in the package. Most of it has no official machine-readable publication, so it was compiled from public third-party sources. This page lists them and says plainly what is verified, what is only consistent with other sources, and what is not checked at all. Where a claim is weaker than it sounds, we say so. Note. Source lookups were done on 2026-10-07 and 2026-10-08. Banks merge and rename, operators reassign ranges and moon sightings differ, so any table can drift. The data is best-effort and is not an official registry; do not use it alone for legal or financial decisions. The two-source rule A bank card BIN, a Sheba bank code or a national-code prefix is included only when at least two independent sources agree. An entry found in one source, or where sources conflict, is left out on purpose. Consequently a missing entry means \"unknown\", never \"invalid\": a valid card can come back with getBankName() equal to null. Some community datasets share ancestry, so agreement is strong evidence but not fully independent confirmation. Only facts (numeric ranges and the names they map to) were taken; no source text or code was copied. The data tables Data Size Sources Status Bank card BINs (BankCard::getBankName()) 39 BINs two agreeing sources among masihgh/iranian-bank-list, ekhtebar.ir, bankavl.com; spot checks against ircards and iranian_banks incomplete by design; the card check itself is the Luhn checksum and does not use this table Sheba bank codes (Sheba::getBankName()) 38 codes persian-tools dataset (via iranian-sheba), pishkhanak.com, bankavl.com, nabzebourse.com, salambank.net incomplete; the Central Bank table was not reachable, so codes 022, 052, 060/090, 061, 063-066, 069, 073, 075, 078, 079 and 095 rest on the first two sources only. The IBAN check itself is ISO 7064 mod-97-10. National-code place of issuance (NationalCode::getLocation()) 547 prefixes three community datasets must agree on province and city (persian-tools, Iranian-national-code-generator, node-iranian-ssn); a fourth (iran-lib) may only veto the three datasets share ancestry; no official list exists. The prefix is the place of issuance, not birth or residence. Other prefixes return null. Mobile operator prefixes (Mobile) well-established blocks only no specific source is cited in the code best-effort. Number portability means a prefix never guarantees the current operator. Umm al-Qura month table (Hijri) AH 1300-1500 (1882-11-12 to 2077-11-16) generated from the ICU/CLDR islamic-umalqura calendar data; only the resulting month lengths are embedded spot-checked against known anchors; not verified month by month against the official KACST publication Prayer-time constants 6 methods, 14 built-in cities Meeus Astronomical Algorithms and the NOAA solar equations; published method angles; approximate public city coordinates see the per-method status below Holidays (IranHolidays) fixed Jalali holidays plus Islamic ones fixed dates are public law and custom; Islamic ones are computed from the Hijri table fixed ones are exact; Islamic ones can be 1-2 days off The data lives in resources/data/*.php and is not public API: its contents may be corrected in any release (see API stability). Provenance details are kept in resources/data/SOURCES.md in the repository. Verification status per feature Feature What was checked What was not checked Jalali conversion An independent implementation of the arithmetic 33-year leap rule. It matches morilog/jalali on every day from 1900-01-01 to 2099-12-27 (73,000 days, 0 mismatches), and matched the previous implementation on every supported day. Both libraries use the same arithmetic rule, so agreement means they are consistent with each other, not that either matches the astronomical Iranian calendar far from the present. Hebrew calendar Pure-PHP algorithm; known-answer tests for Rosh Hashanah 5783 to 5787 and for round trips with Gregorian dates. No exhaustive comparison against an independent implementation is documented. Hijri (Umm al-Qura) Anchors: 1 Ramadan 1446 = 2025-03-01, 1 Muharram 1447 = 2025-06-26, 1 Shawwal 1445 = 2024-04-10. The table is not compared month by month with KACST. Outside AH 1300-1500, and for HijriVariant::Tabular, the date is arithmetic and can differ from observation by a day or two. Iranian holidays Fixed Jalali holidays. Islamic holidays follow the calendar, while Iran announces them by moon sighting: expect 1-2 days of difference. Outside AH 1300-1500 only the fixed holidays are reported. Validators The national-code, Luhn and mod-97 checksums are public algorithms covered by known-answer tests. Bank, operator and place names come from the incomplete tables above. Arabic number words Known-answer tests. Masculine counting form only, below 10^9, no case endings, tanwin or gender agreement. Have a native speaker review formal text. Prayer times: status per method The calculation is clean-room, from standard equations. The only meaningful external check is to compute a city and date and compare with the authority's own published timetable, within the minute rounding of both sides (the tests allow plus or minus 2 minutes). Aggregator websites were not counted, because they use the same published angles and agreement would be circular. Method Status Largest deviation Tehran (Fajr 17.7, Maghrib 4.5) Verified for Fajr, sunrise, Dhuhr and Maghrib against the Institute of Geophysics, University of Tehran tables for 1405 (4 cities, 4 dates). Isha (14 degrees) and Asr are not verified: those tables do not publish them. 1 minute (Shiraz), 0 elsewhere Makkah (Fajr 18.5, Isha 90 min, 120 in Ramadan) Verified against the Umm Al-Qura calendar outside Ramadan (5 dates) and for Ramadan Isha (2 dates). The Ramadan rule (+120 minutes) was a bug that has been fixed. 1 minute Egypt (19.5 / 17.5) Consistent with the Dar al-Ifta Cairo table (3 dates in one month). The page does not name the Survey"},{"t":"Benchmarks","u":"benchmarks.html","d":"A small, honest micro-benchmark of RTLY-Kit against morilog/jalali, with the full method, the caveats and a one-line command to reproduce it on your machine.","g":"Reference","h":[["What is compared","what"],["Results","results"],["Correctness cross-check","correctness"],["Why the difference?","why"],["Reproduce","reproduce"],["Limitations","limitations"]],"x":"Read this first. These are micro-benchmarks of three small scenarios, run once on one machine. They show relative cost on this workload, not a general verdict. Re-run them yourself with the command below before you quote any number. What is compared morilog/jalali v3.5.0 (the most widely used Jalali package) against RTLY-Kit, on three operations: Scenario RTLY-Kit morilog/jalali Gregorian string to Jalali Y/m/d Jalali::make('2025-03-21')->format('Y/m/d') Jalalian::fromDateTime('2025-03-21')->format('Y/m/d') Jalali y/m/d to Gregorian Y-m-d Jalali::create(1404, m, d)->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d') (new Jalalian(1404, m, d))->toCarbon()->format('Y-m-d') addDays then format Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1)->addDays(n)->format('Y/m/d') (new Jalalian(1404, 1, 1))->addDays(n)->format('Y/m/d') The RTLY-Kit side of each scenario, as you would write it: <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\Jalali; echo Jalali::make('2025-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1404/01/01 echo Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1)->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'), \"\\n\"; // 2025-03-21 echo Jalali::create(1404, 1, 1)->addDays(100)->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1404/04/08 Results One run, median of 5 repeats of 20,000 iterations each. Environment: PHP 8.2.30 CLI in Docker on Windows 11, opcache.enable_cli=1. Scenario RTLY-Kit (µs/op) morilog/jalali (µs/op) Ratio Gregorian string to Jalali Y/m/d 13.33 153.84 11.5x Jalali y/m/d to Gregorian Y-m-d 12.80 58.55 4.6x addDays + format 33.18 479.04 14.4x Peak memory for the whole process, with both libraries loaded, was 4 MiB. An earlier ad-hoc run, without opcache and with a different scenario mix, showed a larger gap (roughly 25x). The spread between runs is itself the lesson: the ratio depends on the scenario and on the PHP configuration. Correctness cross-check For every day from 1900-01-01 to 2099-12-27 (73,000 consecutive days) the Jalali date from RTLY-Kit is identical to the one from morilog's CalendarUtils::toJalali: 0 mismatches. Both implement the arithmetic 33-year leap rule, so agreement means that the two are consistent with each other. It does not mean that either matches the official astronomical Iranian calendar far from the present (see Accuracy and data). Why the difference? We have not profiled this, so treat the following as likely, not proven. RTLY-Kit wraps a plain DateTimeImmutable and converts with integer arithmetic. morilog builds and manipulates Carbon objects, which are much larger (parsing and locale machinery), so part of the gap is an architectural choice and not only the conversion algorithm. If you already use Carbon everywhere, the gap that matters to you may be smaller. Reproduce The benchmark lives in tools/benchmark in the repository. Its composer.json installs RTLY-Kit from the working tree plus morilog/jalali in its own vendor/ directory (which is not part of the package). The script prints the PHP version, the opcache and JIT settings and the median of the repeats. docker compose -f tools/docker-compose.yml run --rm php sh -c 'cd tools/benchmark && composer install -n && php -d opcache.enable_cli=1 run.php 20000 5' Numbers from different machines are not comparable. Limitations Three scenarios; no formatting-heavy, parsing-heavy or bulk-array workloads. One machine and one PHP build; Docker on Windows adds noise. No memory-per-object measurement beyond the total peak. Only morilog/jalali was measured; other packages were not."},{"t":"Limits","u":"limits.html","d":"Every hard limit in one place: supported year ranges, input size caps, number-word ranges, the Umm al-Qura range and the other numeric boundaries of the library.","g":"Reference","h":[["Calendar year ranges","calendars"],["Deltas and timestamps","deltas"],["String-year thresholds","string-years"],["Umm al-Qura range","umm-al-qura"],["Input size caps","input-caps"],["Number ranges","numbers"],["Data coverage limits","data"],["Platform limits","platform"]],"x":"The limits below are deliberate. They keep every call cheap and predictable, and keep untrusted input from turning into a wrapped-around date or an expensive computation. Exceeding one always raises a documented RtlyKitException (see Error handling) or, for validators, an invalid Result. Calendar year ranges Every supported date of every calendar maps to a Gregorian year between 1 and 9999, so any value the classes produce can be held by a DateTimeImmutable without wraparound. The ranges are the public constants MIN_YEAR and MAX_YEAR: Calendar MIN_YEAR MAX_YEAR Gregorian date of 1/1 of those years Jalali -620 9377 0001-03-21 .. 9998-03-20 Hijri 1 9665 0622-07-19 .. 9998-10-12 Hebrew 3762 13759 0001-09-06 .. 9998-10-15 <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; use RtlyKit\\Calendar\\{Hebrew, Hijri, Jalali}; foreach ([Jalali::class, Hijri::class, Hebrew::class] as $class) { printf( \"%-7s %5d..%-5d %s .. %s\\n\", (new ReflectionClass($class))->getShortName(), $class::MIN_YEAR, $class::MAX_YEAR, $class::create($class::MIN_YEAR, 1, 1)->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'), $class::create($class::MAX_YEAR, 1, 1)->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'), ); } // Jalali -620..9377 0001-03-21 .. 9998-03-20 // Hijri 1..9665 0622-07-19 .. 9998-10-12 // Hebrew 3762..13759 0001-09-06 .. 9998-10-15 Every entry point obeys the range: make, create, createFromFormat, Unix timestamps, add*() / sub*() (including huge deltas), the helpers and the Carbon macros. A value outside the range throws InvalidDateException; nothing returns a wrapped-around date, and no TypeError or ValueError escapes. Deltas and timestamps An addDays() / subDays() (and hours, minutes, seconds) amount larger than the span of the whole supported calendar (about 3.7 million days, or the same span in hours, minutes or seconds) is rejected up front with InvalidDateException; a smaller amount that lands outside the supported years is rejected as well. A Unix timestamp must fall within Gregorian years 1 to 9999 (with a little slack for the time zone); anything further out is rejected. Hebrew::addMonths() and addYears() run in constant time, however large the amount. String-year thresholds A string such as 1405/01/01 or 1405-01-01 passed to make() or a helper is read as the calendar's own date when the year is below the threshold, and as a Gregorian string otherwise: Class Own-calendar year Otherwise Jalali / jdate() below 1700 Gregorian Hijri / hdate() below 1700 Gregorian Hebrew / hebrew_date() 3000 or more Gregorian To pass a real Gregorian date below the threshold (for example the year 999), use a DateTimeImmutable, which is never reinterpreted. See the FAQ. Umm al-Qura range The embedded Umm al-Qura month table covers AH 1300 to 1500 (1882-11-12 to 2077-11-16). Outside it, and always for HijriVariant::Tabular, Hijri uses arithmetic rules that can differ from observation by a day or two. Hijri::hasUmmAlQuraData($year) tells whether a year is covered. IranHolidays reports Islamic holidays only inside that range. Outside it, only the fixed Jalali holidays are returned. Input size caps Where Limit When exceeded Validators (NationalCode, Sheba, BankCard, Mobile, PostalCode, VehiclePlate) 4096 bytes per string invalid Result, error input_too_long NumberToWords::convert() and fromWords() (string input) 4096 bytes InvalidNumberException, input_too_long Format string input (withSeparator()) 4096 bytes InvalidNumberException, input_too_long Format::withSeparator() / format_number() 1000 characters in the plain decimal number (integer and fraction digits together) InvalidNumberException, input_too_long Slugify::make() separator 64 bytes, valid UTF-8 RtlyKitException (input_too_long / invalid_argument) Jalali::format() pattern 256 bytes (Jalali::MAX_FORMAT_LENGTH) InvalidDateException The caps are part of the contract (see API stability). Number ranges Function Range Beyond it number_to_words($n) (Persian, default) integers up to 21 digits (less than 10^21) and negatives InvalidNumberException, number_too_large number_to_words($n, 'ar') 0 to 999,999,999 and negatives, masculine counting form only InvalidNumberException, number_too_large number_to_words($n, 'de') or any other locale only fa and ar UnsupportedLocaleException NumberToWords::convert(), Format::ordinal() whole numbers; integral floats such as 3.0 are accepted InvalidNumberException for 1.5, NaN, INF Format::ordinal() non-negative integers InvalidNumberException use function RtlyKit\\number_to_words; echo number_to_words(999999999, 'ar'), \"\\n\"; // تسعمئة وتسعة وتسعون مليون وتسعمئة وتسعة وتسعون ألف وتسعمئة وتسعة وتسعون // number_to_words(1000000000, 'ar') throws InvalidNumberException (number_too_large, limit \"10^9\") // number_to_words(str_repeat('9', 22)) throws InvalidNumberException (number_too_large, limit \"10^21 - 1\") Data coverage limits Data Coverage Bank card BINs 39 BINs; others give null from getBankName() Sheba bank codes 38 codes; others give null National-code place of issuance 547 prefixes; others give null Prayer-time cities (PrayerTimes::forCity()) 14: tehran, mashhad, isfahan, shiraz, tabriz, qom, mecca, medina, riyadh, istanbul, cairo, dubai, baghdad, jakarta. For any other place use new PrayerTimes($lat, $lng, ...). Prayer methods Tehran, MWL, ISNA, Egypt, Makkah, Karachi; Asr factor 1 (standard) or 2 (Hanafi) Prayer times at high latitudes a time the sun cannot produce on that day is null, not an error See Accuracy and data for what these tables are based on. Platform limits PHP 8.2 to 8.4 with ext-mbstring; Laravel 11 and 12; Carbon 3. See API stability."},{"t":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"troubleshooting-faq.html","d":"Answers to the questions people actually hit: missing helpers, weekday numbering, string years, time zones, Carbon macros, the Eloquent cast, prayer times and holidays.","g":"Reference","h":[["Setup and helpers","setup"],["I get \"Call to undefined function jdate()\". Where are the helpers?","undefined-function"],["Which exception should I catch?","which-exception"],["Calendars","calendars-faq"],["The weekday number is different for Jalali and Hijri","weekday"],["jdate('1405-01-01') and jdate('2026-03-21') both work, but a Gregorian year like 999 does not","string-year"],["format() prints English digits. How do I get Persian digits?","digits-output"],["jdate('1404/12/30') throws, but 1403/12/30 works","invalid-date"],["\"Today\" is a day off for my users","today-wrong"],["I get an InvalidDateException for a far-future year or a huge number","out-of-range"],["The Hijri date is one day off from my local announcement","hijri-differs"],["Carbon and Laravel","carbon-laravel"],["Call to undefined method toJalali() on a Carbon object","macros-missing"],["The Laravel validation rules (national_code, sheba, ...) are not found","rules-missing"],["My Eloquent model throws InvalidDateException when I assign a date","cast-errors"],["Validators and data","validation-faq"],["is_national_code(13542419) is false, but the string version is true","leading-zero"],["getBankName() returns null for a valid card or Sheba","null-bank"],["Why does a validator never throw, even for null or an array?","what-validators-throw"],["Numbers and text","numbers-faq"],["format_number(1234567.891) prints a long tail of digits","format-float"],["number_to_words() throws for 1.5, for big Arabic numbers and for other languages","words-limits"],["Prayer times and holidays","prayer-faq"],["The prayer times are in the wrong time zone or shifted by an hour","prayer-timezone"],["Some prayer times are null","prayer-null"],["nextPrayer() returned a time that is tomorrow","next-prayer"],["My times differ by a few minutes from my mosque or another app","prayer-differs"],["A religious holiday is a day off from the official calendar","holiday-off"]],"x":"Every answer below was checked against the code, and the examples were run. If your problem is not here, check Error handling (what each exception code means) and Limits first. Setup and helpers I get \"Call to undefined function jdate()\". Where are the helpers? The helpers are namespaced functions in RtlyKit, and the package defines no global function by default, so it can never collide with your code or another package. Import the ones you use, call them by their full name, or opt in to the short global names once at boot: <?php require __DIR__.'/vendor/autoload.php'; use function RtlyKit\\jdate; // option 1: import echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 echo \\RtlyKit\\jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // option 2: full name $skipped = \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(); // option 3: short global names echo \\jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 var_dump($skipped); // array(0) {} (nothing was already taken) Globals::register() never overwrites an existing function and never throws; it returns the names it skipped, so you can log them. In Blade, either call \\RtlyKit\\jdate(...) or register the globals from AppServiceProvider::register(). The Laravel provider does not register them for you. See Helpers and globals. Which exception should I catch? Catch RtlyKit\\Exceptions\\RtlyKitThrowable (or RtlyKitException) for the whole library, or a specific subclass such as InvalidDateException. Validators do not throw at all, so they need no try. Decide by getErrorCode(), not by the message text. Details: Error handling. Calendars The weekday number is different for Jalali and Hijri That is by design. Jalali::getDayOfWeek() (and the w format token) counts from Saturday, the first day of the Persian week: Saturday is 0 and Friday is 6. Hijri and Hebrew count from Sunday like PHP's date('w'), so Saturday is 6. The same day, 2026-03-21 (a Saturday): use function RtlyKit\\{jdate, hdate, hebrew_date}; echo jdate('2026-03-21')->getDayOfWeek(), ' ', hdate('2026-03-21')->getDayOfWeek(), ' ', hebrew_date('2026-03-21')->getDayOfWeek(), \"\\n\"; // 0 6 6 If you want one numbering across calendars, convert with toGregorian()->format('w'). jdate('1405-01-01') and jdate('2026-03-21') both work, but a Gregorian year like 999 does not A string of the form Y/m/d or Y-m-d is read as a date in the class's own calendar when the year is below 1700 (Hijri likewise; Hebrew: a year of 3000 or more), and as a Gregorian string otherwise. So a real Gregorian date with a small year is misread. Pass a DateTimeImmutable instead, which is never reinterpreted: use function RtlyKit\\jdate; echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 (year >= 1700: Gregorian) echo jdate('1405-01-01')->toGregorian()->format('Y-m-d'), \"\\n\"; // 2026-03-21 (year < 1700: Jalali) echo jdate('0999-01-01')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 0999/01/01 (Jalali year 999) echo jdate(new DateTimeImmutable('0999-01-01'))->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 0377/10/11 (Gregorian year 999) Persian and Arabic digits are normalised first, so '۱۴۰۴/۰۱/۰۱' works. Blank strings throw InvalidDateException, and null means \"now\". format() prints English digits. How do I get Persian digits? Formatting returns Latin digits on purpose, so the result is safe for databases and URLs. Convert the output when you display it: use function RtlyKit\\{jdate, to_persian}; echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 echo to_persian(jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d')), \"\\n\"; // ۱۴۰۵/۰۱/۰۱ Hijri::format() can also produce the digits itself through its $digits argument (latin, persian or arabic). jdate('1404/12/30') throws, but 1403/12/30 works Esfand has 30 days only in a leap year. 1403 is a leap year and 1404 is not, so 1404/12/30 does not exist and throws InvalidDateException with the code invalid_date. Month 13, or day 31 in a month of the second half of the year (such as 1404/07/31), behaves the same way. \"Today\" is a day off for my users jdate() with no argument uses PHP's default time zone (date_default_timezone_get(), often UTC on servers). Near midnight Tehran time that is the previous or next Jalali day. Pass a DateTimeZone, or set the default time zone in your application. The timezone argument converts an incoming instant: use function RtlyKit\\jdate; $utc = new DateTimeImmutable('2026-03-20 22:00', new DateTimeZone('UTC')); echo jdate($utc)->format('Y/m/d H:i'), \"\\n\"; // 1404/12/29 22:00 echo jdate($utc, new DateTimeZone('Asia/Tehran'))->format('Y/m/d H:i'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 01:30 I get an InvalidDateException for a far-future year or a huge number Every calendar has a fixed range (Jalali -620 to 9377, Hijri 1 to 9665, Hebrew 3762 to 13759; Gregorian 1 to 9999), and huge add*() amounts and timestamps are refused. This is deliberate: you get an exception, never a wrapped-around date. See Limits. The Hijri date is one day off from my local announcement Hijri uses the Umm al-Qura calendar (the Saudi civil calendar), which is calculated in advance. Local moon-sighting authorities, including Iran's, can start a month a day or two later. The embedded table covers AH 1300 to 1500; outside it, and for HijriVariant::Tabular, the date is arithmetic and can differ from observation by a day or two. See Hijri calendar and Accuracy and data. Carbon and Laravel Call to undefined method toJalali() on a Carbon object The Carbon macros (toJalali, jformat, createFromJalali, toHijri, toHebrew, createFromHijri, createFromHebrew) are registered only when nesbot/carbon (version 3) is installed. Composer lists it as a suggestion, so install it yourself: composer require nesbot/carbon. The macros are registered when the package's helpers file is loaded by Composer's autoloader (and again by the Laravel provider on boot), for Carbon and CarbonImmutable. Laravel's Illuminate\\Support\\Carbon extends Carbon, so it works too: use Carbon\\Carbon; echo Carbon::parse('2026-03-21')->toJalali()->format('Y/m/d'), \"\\n\"; // 1405/01/01 echo"},{"t":"Upgrade guide","u":"upgrade.html","d":"Breaking changes before 1.0 with the exact steps to migrate, plus the highlights of the changelog for the 0.x series.","g":"Reference","h":[["From the pre-release helpers to the namespaced helpers","helpers"],["1. Global helper functions are no longer defined by default","no-globals"],["2. Other changes to be aware of","other-changes"],["Changelog highlights for the 0.x series","changelog-highlights"],["Behaviour that changed","highlights-breaking"],["Added","highlights-added"],["Fixed","highlights-fixed"]],"x":"Before 1.0.0, a minor release may contain breaking changes. Each one is listed here with the steps to migrate. The policy is on API stability. The rest of this page covers the changes that are currently unreleased, that is, everything since the first pre-release builds; no version has been tagged yet. From the pre-release helpers to the namespaced helpers 1. Global helper functions are no longer defined by default Breaking. Earlier pre-release builds defined jdate(), to_persian(), is_national_code() and the other helpers as global functions the moment Composer loaded the package. That could collide with your code or another package, so it is gone. The helpers now live in the RtlyKit namespace and nothing is defined globally. The 27 helpers: jdate, hdate, hebrew_date, to_persian_digits, to_english_digits, to_persian, to_english, is_national_code, is_sheba, is_bank_card, is_mobile, is_postal_code, is_vehicle_plate, validate_national_code, validate_sheba, validate_bank_card, validate_mobile, validate_postal_code, validate_vehicle_plate, number_to_words, normalize_text, contains_rtl, text_direction, is_iran_holiday, prayer_times, format_number and ordinal. Option A: import what you use (recommended). use function RtlyKit\\jdate; use function RtlyKit\\is_national_code; echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'); // 1405/01/01 Or call the fully qualified name: \\RtlyKit\\jdate('2026-03-21'). Option B: restore the old short global names. Call Globals::register() once, for example in your bootstrap file: \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(); echo jdate('2026-03-21')->format('Y/m/d'); // 1405/01/01, works as before register() defines only the names that are still free. It never overwrites an existing function and never throws. It returns the list of names it skipped because something else already uses them, and it is safe to call more than once: $skipped = \\RtlyKit\\Globals::register(); if ($skipped !== []) { error_log('RTLY-Kit globals skipped: '.implode(', ', $skipped)); // use \\RtlyKit\\name() for those } The Laravel service provider does not register globals either. In Blade, write {{ \\RtlyKit\\jdate($post->created_at)->format('j F Y') }}, or call Globals::register() from your AppServiceProvider::register(). More on Helpers and globals. 2. Other changes to be aware of Change What to do Validators take any value. All six implement RtlyKit\\Contracts\\Validator (validate(mixed): Result, isValid(mixed): bool). Non-string input (null, arrays, objects) returns an invalid Result with error invalid_type; input over 4096 bytes returns input_too_long. They never throw for bad input. Remove defensive try blocks and type checks around validators. See Validators overview. Exceptions carry codes. getErrorCode() (an ErrorCode enum) and getContext() exist on every library exception. Nothing to change. Prefer matching on the code over the message text. See Error handling. Slugify::make() throws RtlyKitException (input_too_long or invalid_argument) for an invalid UTF-8 separator or one longer than 64 bytes. Only matters if you pass a user-controlled separator. Catch RtlyKitThrowable. Calendars share a contract. Jalali, Hijri and Hebrew implement RtlyKit\\Contracts\\CalendarDate. Comparisons and diffIn*() accept a CalendarDate or any DateTimeInterface, make() accepts a CalendarDate, and equals(), isBefore(), isAfter() are new aliases. Existing calls keep working. See Convert and compare. Reference data moved to resources/data/*.php. If you read the old table classes directly (they were never public API), switch to public classes such as Hijri, Sheba::getBankName() or NationalCode::getLocation(). Makkah Isha in Ramadan changed. PrayerTimes::METHOD_MAKKAH now returns Isha as Maghrib + 120 minutes during Ramadan (Umm al-Qura practice) and + 90 minutes otherwise. The previous build always used 90. Ramadan Isha moves 30 minutes later. This is a bug fix; update any stored expected values. See the example below. A check of the new Makkah behaviour (2026-03-01 is in Ramadan, 2026-03-21 is not): use RtlyKit\\Prayer\\PrayerTimes; $mecca = PrayerTimes::forCity('mecca', PrayerTimes::METHOD_MAKKAH); foreach (['2026-03-01', '2026-03-21'] as $d) { $t = $mecca->getTimes(new DateTimeImmutable($d)); echo $d, ' maghrib ', $t['maghrib'], ' isha ', $t['isha'], \"\\n\"; } // 2026-03-01 maghrib 18:25 isha 20:25 // 2026-03-21 maghrib 18:32 isha 20:02 Changelog highlights for the 0.x series These are the user-visible points of the changelog, grouped; the repository's CHANGELOG.md has the full list. Behaviour that changed Out-of-range calendar input throws. Years, timestamps and huge add*() / sub*() deltas outside the supported ranges raise InvalidDateException instead of a TypeError or a wrapped-around date. Ranges: Jalali -620 to 9377, Hijri 1 to 9665, Hebrew 3762 to 13759 (Limits). make() string rules are specified. A year below 1700 (Hebrew: 3000 or more) is read in the class's own calendar, everything else as Gregorian; blank strings throw; null is now. A Gregorian ISO string with a year below 1700 is therefore read as Jalali or Hijri: pass a DateTimeImmutable for such dates (FAQ). Holidays. IranHolidays reports Islamic holidays only for AH 1300 to 1500. Outside it, only the fixed Jalali holidays are returned, and a year outside the Jalali range throws InvalidDateException. Number input. NumberToWords::convert() and Format::ordinal() accept integral floats (3.0) and throw InvalidNumberException for fractional floats, NaN and INF. Input caps (security hardening): strings over 4096 bytes are rejected by NumberToWords and Format, and Format::withSeparator() rejects numbers over 1000 characters. They throw InvalidNumberException (input_too_long). Data tables trimmed to confirmed entries. NationalCode::getLocation() covers 547 prefixes and returns null for the rest; bank BIN (39), Sheba code (38) and operator tables keep only entries confirmed by two sources. A name that was returned before may now be null. BankCard rejects numbers made of one repeated digit."}];
